WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.800 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.200 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.612 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.612 --> 00:00:13.570 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Let me go
ahead and get started.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:19.040 align:middle line:84%
So today's topic is called
strategic investment.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.920 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember
where we were last week

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.710 align:middle line:84%
is we were talking about the
effect of entry on markets.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.580 align:middle line:84%
And I made this big
pitch that in some sense,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.140 align:middle line:84%
entry is just
critically important.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.432 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about, what
are firms' actual profits,

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.920 align:middle line:84%
firms' actual profits are
just a rounding error.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.248 align:middle line:84%
And so it's really the
amount of market power

00:00:38.248 --> 00:00:40.790 align:middle line:84%
you have relative to the number
of firms in the market that's

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.100 align:middle line:90%
going to determine profits.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.290 align:middle line:84%
This means that if you
could affect entry,

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.970 align:middle line:84%
it could have a dramatic
effect on how firms are doing.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.620 align:middle line:84%
And I think in that
previous lecture,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.940 align:middle line:84%
it was always just that
there was just fixed costs.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.660 align:middle line:84%
Firms came in, paid the same--
paid some exogenous fixed costs,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.370 align:middle line:90%
and then there was competition.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.950 align:middle line:84%
There's a very, very
large literature

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.830 align:middle line:84%
in economics about
what can firms do

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.910 align:middle line:90%
to change the market structure.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.240 align:middle line:84%
And that can either be actually
changing the market structure

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.940 align:middle line:84%
to try to affect entry or just
changing the market structure

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.400 align:middle line:84%
to try to affect what the
competitive game looks

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.700 align:middle line:84%
like if you can't stop
other firms from entering.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you look
at Tirole's book,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.190 align:middle line:84%
the chapter on strategic
entry deterrence

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.000 align:middle line:90%
is by far the largest chapter.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.860 align:middle line:84%
And it's many more things
written in the 35 years

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.530 align:middle line:90%
since then.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.880 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, many of
these models are

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.850 align:middle line:90%
the classic three-stage game.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.680 align:middle line:84%
In the first stage, firm 1
chooses some investment k1.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.800 align:middle line:90%
It pays some sunk cost c of k1.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.640 align:middle line:90%
And.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.680 align:middle line:84%
When I use the word investment,
I mean investment very broadly.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.470 align:middle line:84%
The important things
about the investment

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.850 align:middle line:84%
is that it some sunk
cost of making it,

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:03.320 align:middle line:84%
and it's credible that you're
not going to be able to undo it.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.230 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance
building capacity,

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.630 align:middle line:84%
if you build a new
factory, you've

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.367 align:middle line:84%
built the new factory you're not
going to unbuild the factory.

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:12.950 align:middle line:84%
And so if you build
a new factory that

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.140 align:middle line:84%
lets you produce a
lower cost you've

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.570 align:middle line:84%
then committed yourself to
being able to produce a lower

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.310 align:middle line:90%
cost in the future.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.170 align:middle line:84%
If you advertise, you
spend money advertising,

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.900 align:middle line:84%
you've changed the
consumer's willingness

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.400 align:middle line:84%
to pay for your
good and willingness

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.970 align:middle line:84%
to pay for other
goods in the market,

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.750 align:middle line:84%
you can't unadvertise
and get the money back

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.960 align:middle line:84%
and then say, OK, now the
other firm's done something.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.640 align:middle line:84%
I'll unadvertise,
get my money back,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.820 align:middle line:84%
and let the preferences go
back to where they were.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.480 align:middle line:84%
So anything that
has that feature

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:51.060 align:middle line:84%
that you make some initial
choice, it incurs a cost,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.460 align:middle line:84%
and it's hard to undo,
that's an investment.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.150 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, like
designing an iPhone

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.950 align:middle line:84%
with a lightning charger
instead of a USB-C charger

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:02.910 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.050 align:middle line:90%
You can't undesign the iPhone.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.410 align:middle line:84%
You could design a new iPhone
that costs you more money,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.368 align:middle line:84%
but you've got the old
ones out there that have

00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:13.050 align:middle line:90%
that particular port in them.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.050 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 observes the
investment level

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.240 align:middle line:84%
and then decides
whether to enter.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.490 align:middle line:84%
If it enters,
there's a cost of E.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to do this in the
monopoly-going-to-duopoly case.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.610 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, you could do all
this with two firms, three firms

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.030 align:middle line:90%
going to n plus 1 firms.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.760 align:middle line:84%
And then if firm 2
stays out of the market,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.980 align:middle line:90%
firm 1 becomes a monopolist.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.850 align:middle line:84%
It chooses some action, a1,
like a price or a quantity,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.400 align:middle line:84%
and then gets a profit that
depends on the investment

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.080 align:middle line:90%
and on the action.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.770 align:middle line:84%
And if firm 2 enters,
they simultaneously

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:44.850 align:middle line:90%
choose these actions.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.460 align:middle line:84%
And they earn profits that
depend on the investment

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.990 align:middle line:90%
and on both actions.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:52.270 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to assume
that this game that

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.750 align:middle line:84%
follows in second stage has
well-behaved profit functions,

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.690 align:middle line:84%
and it has a unique Nash
equilibrium, where the Nash

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:01.650 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium depends on k1.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.570 align:middle line:90%


00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And you'll typically-- if the
action is an investment that

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.420 align:middle line:84%
firm 1 takes in
reducing its costs,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.010 align:middle line:90%
the prices that they set are--

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.310 align:middle line:84%
firm 1's action obviously
reflects its costs.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.200 align:middle line:84%
But then firm 2's
action is also going

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.630 align:middle line:84%
to reflect firm 1's costs
because firm 2 knows

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:28.480 align:middle line:84%
that firm 1's investment is
going to reduce its cost,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.580 align:middle line:90%
leading to change its price.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.010 align:middle line:84%
There can also be direct effects
of the investment on both firms.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.460 align:middle line:84%
So in the advertising
case, the advertising

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.968 align:middle line:84%
can be changing the demand
for firm 2's product

00:04:38.968 --> 00:04:40.510 align:middle line:84%
or the valuation of
firm 2's product,

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:44.560 align:middle line:84%
as well as the valuation
of firm 1's product.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.550 align:middle line:84%
So in the early
literature on this,

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.220 align:middle line:84%
Joe Bain described three
different situations

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.170 align:middle line:84%
that you could see
in an industry.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.820 align:middle line:84%
The first one he described
as blockaded entry.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.540 align:middle line:84%
In the blockaded
entry case, firms

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.420 align:middle line:84%
2's entry costs are sufficiently
high that firm 2 is never

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:04.270 align:middle line:84%
going to enter if firm
1 just ignores it.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.040 align:middle line:84%
So in the blockaded
entry case, firm 1

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.165 align:middle line:84%
just is-- essentially this
is the monopoly model.

00:05:09.165 --> 00:05:09.790 align:middle line:90%
You just go on.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.140 align:middle line:84%
You choose the
optimal investment,

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:12.220 align:middle line:84%
and then you earn
the profits you

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.012 align:middle line:84%
get with the optimal
investment, and firm 2

00:05:14.012 --> 00:05:16.510 align:middle line:90%
is basically irrelevant.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:20.180 align:middle line:84%
Second case he talks about
is the deterred entry case.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.380 align:middle line:84%
So suppose entry costs
are a little bit lower

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:24.880 align:middle line:84%
than in the
blockaded entry case,

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.135 align:middle line:84%
so that if firm 1 ignores
firm 2, firm 2 will enter.

00:05:30.135 --> 00:05:31.510 align:middle line:84%
But then he argues
that there are

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:35.860 align:middle line:84%
going to be some cases where the
firm can actively deter entry,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.650 align:middle line:84%
where the firm can distort its
investment in some way that

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.500 align:middle line:84%
reduces firm 2's profits, that
firm 2 will no longer want

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.310 align:middle line:84%
to enter, and firm 1
remains a monopolist.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.580 align:middle line:84%
And then the third case he
called accommodated entry.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.740 align:middle line:84%
Deterring entry is
either impossible

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.150 align:middle line:84%
or it's just so costly you
wouldn't want to do it.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.360 align:middle line:84%
So the monopolist
chooses its investment

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:58.930 align:middle line:84%
not to keep firm 2 out, but to
change the nature of competition

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.090 align:middle line:84%
in some way that's going to earn
it higher post-entry profits.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:08.832 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to focus
on two main questions here.

00:06:08.832 --> 00:06:11.040 align:middle line:84%
Essentially we're going to
ignore the blockaded entry

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.410 align:middle line:84%
case except as a
counterpoint and then

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.200 align:middle line:84%
ask, when do firms want
to deter entry versus try

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.680 align:middle line:90%
to accommodate entry?

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.990 align:middle line:84%
And how do you use the
investment, k, to do that?

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.180 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, the
answer turns out

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.370 align:middle line:84%
to depend on how you
use your investment.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.040 align:middle line:84%
It depends on what type
of investment it is

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.670 align:middle line:90%
and what type of game it is.

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.510 align:middle line:84%
And Fudenberg and Tirole
have this paper from 1984

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.030 align:middle line:84%
that goes over a simple
classification of types of games

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.440 align:middle line:90%
and types of investments.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.370 align:middle line:84%
So I will do that before I
discuss some applications.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:45.160 align:middle line:84%
So your paper discusses
two types of games.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.160 align:middle line:84%
I think I've done this earlier,
mentioned these terms earlier.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.450 align:middle line:84%
We say that a game has strategic
complements if the best response

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:53.460 align:middle line:84%
correspondences
are upward sloping.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.290 align:middle line:84%
And it has strategic
substitutes, actually,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.790 align:middle line:84%
if there are curves and if
they're downward sloping.

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:59.670 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember
the Hotelling model,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:00.970 align:middle line:90%
you graph the best responses.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.290 align:middle line:90%
It looks like this.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.150 align:middle line:84%
The Cornell model, you
graph the best responses.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:06.015 align:middle line:90%
They look like that.

00:07:06.015 --> 00:07:10.570 align:middle line:90%


00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.130 align:middle line:90%
Why was that?

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:15.840 align:middle line:90%
This is a simple theorem.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.090 align:middle line:84%
You could do this more generally
using increasing differences

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:19.900 align:middle line:90%
terminology.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.320 align:middle line:84%
But a game is generally going
to have strategic complements

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.890 align:middle line:84%
if the second cross partial,
second derivative of profit

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:30.190 align:middle line:84%
with respect to ai and aj
is positive and strategic

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.370 align:middle line:84%
substitutes if the second
partial of profits with effect

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.910 align:middle line:90%
to ai and aj is negative.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:38.530 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, you
know, we usually

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:41.140 align:middle line:84%
think of price competition
as being the complements case

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:45.700 align:middle line:84%
and quantity competition as
being the substitutes case.

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:50.410 align:middle line:84%
But I would note here that the
strategic complements and price

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:54.840 align:middle line:84%
competition is only there,
has been there in all

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.590 align:middle line:84%
our previous analyses,
because I've always

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.320 align:middle line:84%
been using linear demand
curves that are separable.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:03.800 align:middle line:84%
I'm using demand curves
that look like D of pi,

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:11.230 align:middle line:84%
pj is 1/2 plus pj minus pi over
2t or something like that where

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:15.460 align:middle line:84%
there's no interaction
between pi and pj,

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.910 align:middle line:84%
because if you look at the
profit function in a price

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.370 align:middle line:90%
competition game, you take the--

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:24.040 align:middle line:84%
I first differentiate
with respect to pi.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:29.350 align:middle line:84%
And I get pi minus c,
dDi dpi plus D of pi.

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:33.460 align:middle line:84%
And then I differentiate
this with respect to pj.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:37.610 align:middle line:84%
And then I get-- this
doesn't depend on pj,

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.890 align:middle line:84%
so I get p minus c, second
derivative of demand

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.940 align:middle line:84%
with respect to pi
and pj plus dDi, dpj.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:47.840 align:middle line:84%
And we're used to this term,
firm j increases its price,

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.140 align:middle line:90%
firm i's demand goes up.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:51.540 align:middle line:90%
This is the positive term.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:56.040 align:middle line:84%
But then there is this
second term, pi minus c

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.930 align:middle line:84%
times this second derivative of
Di with respect to pi and pj.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:07.920 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, if I had an
interaction in this demand

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.510 align:middle line:84%
curve, then this term
would be negative.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:13.510 align:middle line:84%
This term would be negative
multiplied by this.

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:16.200 align:middle line:84%
It's no longer obvious you
have strategic complements.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.040 align:middle line:84%
So normally we think
of price competition

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.840 align:middle line:84%
as complements and quantity
competition as substitutes,

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.635 align:middle line:90%
but that's not always true.

00:09:24.635 --> 00:09:29.580 align:middle line:90%


00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.760 align:middle line:84%
OK, second thing we're going
to have to do in this model

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.460 align:middle line:84%
is classify types
of investments.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.587 align:middle line:84%
And the way the investment
classification is going to work

00:09:38.587 --> 00:09:40.170 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to
classify investments

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:43.890 align:middle line:84%
based on their effect on
the potential entrant.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.670 align:middle line:84%
So in this model, I'm going to
define this reduced form profit

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.760 align:middle line:84%
function to be the
profit that firm I

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.820 align:middle line:90%
gets when firm 1 has chosen k1.

00:09:54.820 --> 00:10:01.010 align:middle line:84%
And then we play the
equilibrium action following k1.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:06.120 align:middle line:84%
And so a simple definition,
investment makes firm 1 tough,

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.030 align:middle line:90%
essentially if it hurts firm 2.

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:11.900 align:middle line:84%
If this reduced form profit
function is decreasing in k,

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.470 align:middle line:84%
investment makes firm 1
soft if that reduced form

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:19.430 align:middle line:90%
profit is increasing in k.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.310 align:middle line:84%
And you know,
sometimes it's obvious

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.550 align:middle line:90%
which this is going to be.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:26.510 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, if this is an
investment in cost reduction,

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.450 align:middle line:84%
your intuition tells you if
firm 1 has lower costs, that's

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:31.940 align:middle line:84%
going to be bad for firm
2 in equilibrium when

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:33.510 align:middle line:90%
the prices shake out.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:38.010 align:middle line:84%
And so we will have d
pi 2 dk1 less than 0,

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.370 align:middle line:84%
so cost reductions
in investment,

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.830 align:middle line:90%
that makes you tough.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:46.550 align:middle line:84%
If the investment is
lobbying to lower the tax

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:51.920 align:middle line:84%
rates in an industry, if
it's just like a profits tax,

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.600 align:middle line:84%
a percentage tax on
profits, it won't

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.250 align:middle line:90%
affect any of the behavior.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.802 align:middle line:84%
So if you lobby to get taxes
reduced, a reduction in taxes

00:10:58.802 --> 00:11:00.510 align:middle line:84%
isn't going to change
any of the actions.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.860 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to end up
being good for both firms.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:07.140 align:middle line:84%
So lobbying to lower tax
rates would be an investment

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.320 align:middle line:90%
that makes you soft.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.710 align:middle line:84%
When it's not
obvious, it can help

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:17.770 align:middle line:84%
to write down what this
profit function looks like.

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.720 align:middle line:84%
So if I think about
differentiating,

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:30.780 align:middle line:84%
so I have pi 2 star
of k1 is profit

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:37.940 align:middle line:84%
that firm 2 gets when
the investment is k1.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.313 align:middle line:84%
And we have a1 star
of k1, a2 star of k1.

00:11:41.313 --> 00:11:46.170 align:middle line:90%


00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.660 align:middle line:84%
OK, if I differentiate
this with respect to k1,

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.020 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to get three terms.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.830 align:middle line:84%
First, I get the
derivative here,

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:57.800 align:middle line:84%
so I get the partial of
pi 2 with respect to k1.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:02.480 align:middle line:84%
Second, I get this effect that
when I change k1, a1 changes.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.450 align:middle line:84%
And that change in
a1 affects pi 2.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:09.590 align:middle line:84%
And so that's partial pi
2 with respect to a1 times

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:13.430 align:middle line:84%
partial a1 star
with respect to k1.

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:14.720 align:middle line:90%
And then there's also--

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.553 align:middle line:84%
you would think there
would be a third term.

00:12:16.553 --> 00:12:18.350 align:middle line:84%
When I change k1,
it affects this.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:20.270 align:middle line:90%
And that affects profits.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:27.590 align:middle line:84%
But the third term would be
partial pi 2 with respect

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:35.840 align:middle line:84%
to a2 evaluated at
a2 star of k1 times

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:46.520 align:middle line:84%
derivative of a2 star with
respect to k1 evaluated at k1.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:51.600 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about
this term here, that's 0.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.930 align:middle line:84%
When you evaluate d pi 2 / da2
at a2 star of k1, a2 star of k1

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.630 align:middle line:84%
was chosen where the first
order condition is 0.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:04.630 align:middle line:84%
So there's no effect
from k1 changes--

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:09.740 align:middle line:84%
there's no third effect from
k1 leads firm 2 to change a2.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:11.600 align:middle line:84%
And that has an
effect on profits.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:15.340 align:middle line:84%
The marginal effect of
changing a2 on profits is 0.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:18.010 align:middle line:84%
So we really-- so
this term is 0.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.610 align:middle line:90%
And we just have two effects.

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:23.410 align:middle line:84%
We have what's called the
direct effect where k1 directly

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:27.640 align:middle line:84%
affects firm 2's profits, and
for the investment like cost

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:30.820 align:middle line:90%
reduction, this term is 0.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.220 align:middle line:84%
Firm 1's cost reduction
has no direct effect

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:34.760 align:middle line:90%
on firm 2's profits.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:38.440 align:middle line:84%
And in the cost reduction case,
it comes in through this term

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:42.160 align:middle line:84%
that when I invest
more in cost reduction,

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.810 align:middle line:90%
I lower firm 1's price.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:45.700 align:middle line:90%
So this is negative.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:49.810 align:middle line:84%
When I lower my price, d
pi 2 / da1 is positive.

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:53.750 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 benefits when firm
1 sets a higher price.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:56.420 align:middle line:84%
So the cost reduction has a
negative strategic effect.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:59.260 align:middle line:84%
It causes firm 1 to
do something that's

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:03.370 align:middle line:84%
bad for firm 2, which
is cut its price.

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:05.950 align:middle line:84%
In the lobbying to
lower tax rates,

00:14:05.950 --> 00:14:09.020 align:middle line:84%
there I was describing a thing
that has a direct effect.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.480 align:middle line:84%
If I lobbied lower
tax rates and I

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:13.210 align:middle line:84%
get tax rates lowered,
holding actions fixed,

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:15.410 align:middle line:90%
that raises firm 2's profits.

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:18.970 align:middle line:84%
And then if it's just
a tax on profits,

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:22.430 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't affect
equilibrium prices.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:25.365 align:middle line:84%
So da1 / dk1 is 0, and
there's no strategic effect.

00:14:25.365 --> 00:14:29.020 align:middle line:90%


00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.560 align:middle line:84%
And so I have some
examples on the other side.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:34.690 align:middle line:90%
You can think about--

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:38.920 align:middle line:84%
so for instance, suppose we had
a competition on a line game.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And firm 1 has the option to
extend the line and move even

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:45.460 align:middle line:84%
further away out
in this direction.

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:46.725 align:middle line:90%
What would that do?

00:14:46.725 --> 00:14:49.877 align:middle line:90%


00:14:49.877 --> 00:14:51.460 align:middle line:84%
There, they're going
to be-- and let's

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:52.710 align:middle line:90%
have it in the hotel and game.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.190 align:middle line:90%
So firm 1 used to be here.

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:56.320 align:middle line:84%
And firm 1 is a chance
to just move further away

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.530 align:middle line:90%
off the end of the beach.

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:03.650 align:middle line:84%
So moving further away, the
direct effect is positive.

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:07.780 align:middle line:84%
If we hold the prices as fixed
and firm 1 moves farther away,

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:10.210 align:middle line:84%
the midpoint of the line
moves from here to here,

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:13.860 align:middle line:84%
so firm 2 is going to
get more customers.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And then the strategic
effect, again,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.910 align:middle line:84%
profit of firm 2 with respect
to price of firm 1 is positive.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.030 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 likes 1 to
set high prices.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.310 align:middle line:84%
And the further
firm 1 moves away,

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.140 align:middle line:84%
the higher will
firm 1's price be.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.990 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, this is
positive, this is positive.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.610 align:middle line:84%
So the direct effect in
that model is positive.

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.143 align:middle line:84%
The strategic
effect is positive.

00:15:35.143 --> 00:15:36.810 align:middle line:84%
Moving away from firm
2 is an investment

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:37.910 align:middle line:90%
that makes firm 1 soft.

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.875 align:middle line:90%


00:15:40.875 --> 00:15:43.990 align:middle line:84%
And other things--
investments can be neither.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.947 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about
an advertising campaign,

00:15:46.947 --> 00:15:48.780 align:middle line:84%
suppose you run an
advertising campaign that

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:50.430 align:middle line:84%
will convince some
number of customers

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.020 align:middle line:84%
to never leave your
firm or to have

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:56.190 align:middle line:84%
a tremendous loyalty for your
firm and not want to switch.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:59.290 align:middle line:84%
The direct effect on
your rival is negative.

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:00.833 align:middle line:84%
When those customers
become captive,

00:16:00.833 --> 00:16:02.500 align:middle line:84%
they're not going to
consider the rival.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:04.625 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the rival is
going to get fewer of them,

00:16:04.625 --> 00:16:05.830 align:middle line:90%
holding prices fixed.

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:08.400 align:middle line:90%
That hurts the rival.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:11.910 align:middle line:84%
But again, price
competition, firm 2

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:13.868 align:middle line:90%
likes me charging high prices.

00:16:13.868 --> 00:16:15.660 align:middle line:84%
And when I have these
captive customers who

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:17.118 align:middle line:84%
won't consider
switching, I'm going

00:16:17.118 --> 00:16:19.090 align:middle line:84%
to price high to exploit
the captive customers.

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:22.030 align:middle line:84%
I can't discriminate between the
captive and non-captive people.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.390 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, my price
is going to be higher.

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:25.980 align:middle line:84%
So you get a negative
direct effect,

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:27.580 align:middle line:90%
a positive strategic effect.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.735 align:middle line:90%
It's not clear.

00:16:29.735 --> 00:16:31.110 align:middle line:84%
It would depend
on which of those

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:32.490 align:middle line:84%
is larger whether this
is an investment that

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:33.610 align:middle line:90%
makes you tough or soft.

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:34.860 align:middle line:90%
And it could be neither.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:40.010 align:middle line:84%
It could be sometimes soft in
some ranges, tough in others.

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:43.000 align:middle line:84%
But just the tough, soft
distinction is all about--

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.430 align:middle line:84%
it's not really how
it affects firm 1.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:46.665 align:middle line:90%
It's how it affects firm 2.

00:16:46.665 --> 00:16:52.290 align:middle line:90%


00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:55.270 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me now go through
Bain's cases in order,

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:58.260 align:middle line:84%
so first, this
blockaded entry case.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:02.130 align:middle line:84%
So blockaded entry case is
the case where firm 2 just

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.140 align:middle line:84%
has very, very high entry
costs and will never

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.819 align:middle line:90%
consider entering.

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:09.030 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
write k1m and a1m

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:12.130 align:middle line:84%
for what a monopolist would do
if there wasn't even a firm 2.

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.060 align:middle line:84%
It's just going to
choose the a1 and the k1

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:19.290 align:middle line:84%
to maximize its monopoly
profit function.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.780 align:middle line:84%
And the blockaded
entry case is when,

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:26.190 align:middle line:84%
if you choose this optimal
monopoly investment,

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.950 align:middle line:84%
firm 2's equilibrium
profits are less than e,

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.150 align:middle line:90%
so firm 2 is not going to enter.

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:33.630 align:middle line:84%
And so then this is-- really,
it's not an entry model.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:36.100 align:middle line:84%
Firm 1 just ignores
potential entrant.

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.530 align:middle line:90%
It chooses k1m.

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:38.555 align:middle line:90%
That's all that happens.

00:17:38.555 --> 00:17:43.600 align:middle line:90%


00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:45.400 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned that,
because now let's

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.733 align:middle line:84%
think about what happens if
you're going to deter entry.

00:17:47.733 --> 00:17:49.910 align:middle line:90%


00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:53.163 align:middle line:84%
The optimal entry
deterring investment

00:17:53.163 --> 00:17:55.080 align:middle line:84%
is going-- you're going
to solve this problem.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:58.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to try to maximize
over all possible investment

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:01.190 align:middle line:84%
levels and actions
the profit that I'm

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:05.900 align:middle line:84%
going to get from choosing k1
and then choosing action a1.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:08.660 align:middle line:84%
But I've got to do it
subject to the constraint

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:11.240 align:middle line:84%
that firm 2's
equilibrium profits

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:16.290 align:middle line:84%
after I've chosen k1 are at most
e, so that firm 2 doesn't enter.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:19.075 align:middle line:84%
So I'm choosing this same
maximization problem,

00:18:19.075 --> 00:18:20.450 align:middle line:84%
but I just put in
this constraint

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:26.780 align:middle line:84%
that I have to ensure that
firm 2's profit is less than e.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:31.100 align:middle line:84%
And the entry deterrence
case I'm looking at

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:42.140 align:middle line:84%
is suppose that pi 2 star
of k1m is greater than e.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:44.820 align:middle line:84%
So in this case,
firm 2's profits,

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.870 align:middle line:84%
if I just behave as
if firm 2 isn't there,

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:49.800 align:middle line:90%
firm 2 is going to enter.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:51.803 align:middle line:90%
So this would be--

00:18:51.803 --> 00:18:54.470 align:middle line:84%
if this was less than e, I would
be in the blockaded entry case.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:56.780 align:middle line:84%
With this greater
than e, this is when I

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:58.220 align:middle line:90%
need to be at this constraint.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:18:59.545 align:middle line:90%
This constraint is binding.

00:18:59.545 --> 00:19:04.410 align:middle line:90%


00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:07.170 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, how do you
distort your investment

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:10.290 align:middle line:90%
and make k1ED differ from k1m?

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:15.030 align:middle line:84%
Very simple theorem says that if
investment makes firm 1 tough,

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:18.000 align:middle line:84%
then the entry deterring
level of investment

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.800 align:middle line:90%
is greater than k1m.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.990 align:middle line:84%
And if investment
makes firm 1 soft,

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:25.595 align:middle line:84%
then the entry deterring level
of investment is less than k1m.

00:19:25.595 --> 00:19:32.500 align:middle line:90%


00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:36.450 align:middle line:84%
An easy way to think about
this is you just have--

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:39.060 align:middle line:84%
I put my investment
on this axis.

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.210 align:middle line:84%
And let me graph some
profits on this axis.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:48.000 align:middle line:84%
So let this be e,
the level of profits

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:51.270 align:middle line:84%
that firm 2 needs to enter
to come into the market.

00:19:51.270 --> 00:20:01.210 align:middle line:84%
And so imagine-- you
know, imagine this is k1m

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:04.780 align:middle line:90%
and this is pi 2 star of k1.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:08.100 align:middle line:84%
So this is an investment
that makes firm 1 tough,

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.093 align:middle line:90%
so investing, investing more--

00:20:11.093 --> 00:20:13.960 align:middle line:90%


00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:18.160 align:middle line:90%
investing more hurts firm 2.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:21.040 align:middle line:90%
And again, if I also graph--

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:25.140 align:middle line:84%
let's imagine pi
1 looks like this.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:29.250 align:middle line:90%


00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:33.490 align:middle line:84%
pi 1 star of k1, it's
maximized at k1 monopoly.

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.030 align:middle line:84%
So what I would like to
do if firm 2 wasn't there

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.730 align:middle line:84%
is I would choose this
level of investment.

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:42.550 align:middle line:84%
I would receive that
level of profits.

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:44.410 align:middle line:84%
But I can't do that,
because if I did,

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:45.840 align:middle line:90%
that, firm 2 would come in.

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:47.620 align:middle line:90%
So this is pi 1.

00:20:47.620 --> 00:20:52.420 align:middle line:84%
We should call this
pi 1 monopoly of k1.

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:53.870 align:middle line:90%
That's what I would like to do.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:54.890 align:middle line:90%
I can't.

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:56.030 align:middle line:90%
What do I have to do?

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.000 align:middle line:84%
I have to choose a
level of investment

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:03.190 align:middle line:84%
that is sufficient to make sure
that pi 2 star is less than e.

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:04.660 align:middle line:84%
So what I would
do in this example

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:09.650 align:middle line:84%
is this would be k1
entry deterrence.

00:21:09.650 --> 00:21:11.000 align:middle line:90%
I just need to--

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:14.300 align:middle line:90%
I need to move firm 2's--

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.400 align:middle line:84%
I need to move firm 2's
profits in whatever--

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:18.025 align:middle line:84%
I need to move k1 in
whatever direction

00:21:18.025 --> 00:21:20.330 align:middle line:90%
moves firm 2's profits down.

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:24.050 align:middle line:84%
And so in this case, I
distort my investment

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:25.260 align:middle line:90%
going in that direction.

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:26.730 align:middle line:90%
And so I'm over-investing.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:30.365 align:middle line:84%
We'll call that I'm
over-investing in k1 in order

00:21:30.365 --> 00:21:31.740 align:middle line:84%
to keep firm 2
out of the market.

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:35.540 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, this
would be the graph of I'm

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:38.420 align:middle line:84%
investing in a
cost-reducing technology.

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:41.270 align:middle line:84%
And so if I-- if
firm 2 wasn't there,

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:44.543 align:middle line:84%
I would just go ahead and do
minimal investments in cost.

00:21:44.543 --> 00:21:46.460 align:middle line:84%
Do this level of investment
in cost reduction,

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:47.970 align:middle line:90%
I'd earn this profits.

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:50.100 align:middle line:84%
But then with this,
if I have the cost

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:53.000 align:middle line:84%
that this investment produces,
firm 2 is going to enter.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:55.490 align:middle line:84%
So what I do is I do
more cost reduction

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:59.060 align:middle line:90%
than I need to as a monopolist.

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:03.080 align:middle line:84%
I'm distorting my
monopoly investment,

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:05.430 align:middle line:84%
therefore, I'm
lowering my profits.

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:07.538 align:middle line:84%
But I do that
because I need to do

00:22:07.538 --> 00:22:10.080 align:middle line:84%
that to keep firm 2 out of the
market and remain a monopolist

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.570 align:middle line:84%
and not be a duopoly, which
in this case, I'm assuming

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:13.700 align:middle line:90%
is even worse for you.

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:17.000 align:middle line:90%


00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:19.280 align:middle line:84%
And if the investment
was one that

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:25.610 align:middle line:84%
made firm 1 soft, if
firm 2's profits instead

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:28.250 align:middle line:84%
looked like this,
then obviously again,

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.320 align:middle line:84%
I would just distort
down in this direction

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:31.850 align:middle line:90%
and take this point.

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:35.900 align:middle line:84%
So I'm always just
distorting in whichever

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:37.950 align:middle line:90%
direction hurts firm 2.

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:40.860 align:middle line:84%
And so strategic entry
deterrence is very simple.

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.480 align:middle line:84%
It's just about
hurting your rival.

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:43.585 align:middle line:90%
That's all there is to it.

00:22:43.585 --> 00:22:49.450 align:middle line:90%


00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:54.520 align:middle line:84%
As I drew this, I made firm 1's
monopoly profits single peaked.

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:56.880 align:middle line:84%
When firm 1's monopoly
profits are single peaked,

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.680 align:middle line:84%
you want to go as
far away from--

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:03.400 align:middle line:84%
you want to stay as close as you
can to k1m and not distort more

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:04.190 align:middle line:90%
than you need to.

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:05.857 align:middle line:84%
So you would always
go just to the point

00:23:05.857 --> 00:23:07.910 align:middle line:84%
where the entry
cost first hits e.

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:13.030 align:middle line:90%


00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:14.475 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

00:23:14.475 --> 00:23:21.540 align:middle line:90%


00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.600 align:middle line:90%
OK, so some examples.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:26.310 align:middle line:84%
In the cost reduction
game, I said,

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:28.980 align:middle line:84%
you know, firm 1 is going to
over-invest in cost reduction

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:31.860 align:middle line:90%
to deter entry.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:33.990 align:middle line:84%
Advertising to increase
market size, so

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:36.600 align:middle line:84%
suppose you have a
new product, you're

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.700 align:middle line:84%
advertising for the
product, and advertising

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:43.950 align:middle line:84%
is going to raise demand for
that product for everyone.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:48.210 align:middle line:84%
And so if this advertising
is going to make more people

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:52.710 align:middle line:84%
want whatever this product is,
if that's going to help you

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:55.410 align:middle line:84%
and that's going to help
rivals, if the rivals enter,

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:58.680 align:middle line:84%
then you're going to
limit that advertising

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:01.770 align:middle line:90%
to avoid attracting entry.

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:03.450 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the
case where investing

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:10.590 align:middle line:84%
in something that has spillovers
to you or your rivals raises--

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:13.590 align:middle line:84%
if it raises everyone's
profits, then increasing

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:16.065 align:middle line:84%
the size of the market, then
it's going to attract entry

00:24:16.065 --> 00:24:17.940 align:middle line:84%
and therefore that
investment makes you soft,

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:20.550 align:middle line:84%
you do less of it
to deter entry.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:24.600 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, if I'm the
Chipotle in Kendall Square and I

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:27.600 align:middle line:84%
just advertise "Burritos
are great" more generally,

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.840 align:middle line:84%
could be a second burrito shop
opens up in Kendall Square

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:34.270 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, everyone likes
burritos more, whatever.

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:37.620 align:middle line:84%
So maybe it's not so
plausible, but you just

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.872 align:middle line:84%
do less advertising, because
the more you advertise,

00:24:39.872 --> 00:24:41.580 align:middle line:84%
the bigger is the
market, the more likely

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.440 align:middle line:84%
some other firm is
going to come in.

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:48.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, differentiation-- if you
can have some way that you can,

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:53.140 align:middle line:84%
either by designing your product
or by advertising your product,

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:55.390 align:middle line:84%
make consumers have
idiosyncratic preference

00:24:55.390 --> 00:25:00.360 align:middle line:84%
for your product relative
to rivals' products,

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:04.530 align:middle line:84%
differentiating also will raise
the-- differentiation will--

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:06.990 align:middle line:84%
in c plus t, if
you increase t, it

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.820 align:middle line:90%
will raise the rivals' profits.

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:12.680 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you want to avoid
differentiating to deter entry.

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:15.470 align:middle line:90%


00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:18.980 align:middle line:84%
Like in a competition
on a line model,

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:21.200 align:middle line:84%
it could be that the
thing I want to do

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:23.797 align:middle line:84%
is locate exactly in the middle,
so that if I locate exactly

00:25:23.797 --> 00:25:26.130 align:middle line:84%
in the middle, then firm 2
has to be pretty close to me,

00:25:26.130 --> 00:25:27.570 align:middle line:90%
and then we don't earn profits.

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:29.665 align:middle line:84%
That keeps the firm
out of the market.

00:25:29.665 --> 00:25:33.670 align:middle line:90%


00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:35.540 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:40.620 align:middle line:90%


00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:44.820 align:middle line:84%
So accommodating
entry, what's going

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:47.730 align:middle line:84%
to happen in this game is
if you think about this,

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:55.295 align:middle line:84%
suppose I reduce e, and
I make e a lot lower.

00:25:55.295 --> 00:25:58.240 align:middle line:90%


00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:00.730 align:middle line:84%
If e is a lot
lower, the only way

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:05.920 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to keep firm 2 out
of the market is to reduce,

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:09.100 align:middle line:84%
is to increase k1 all
the way up to this level.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.560 align:middle line:84%
And then at this level,
as I've drawn it,

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:13.935 align:middle line:90%
my profits are essentially 0.

00:26:13.935 --> 00:26:16.650 align:middle line:90%


00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:19.810 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm going to have--
this was my monopoly profits.

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:22.625 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to also have, say,
like duopoly profits curve.

00:26:22.625 --> 00:26:27.750 align:middle line:90%


00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.270 align:middle line:84%
And if the amount
by which I have

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:37.800 align:middle line:84%
to raise my investment in order
to keep firm 2 out of the market

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:41.610 align:middle line:84%
is so large that it's
going to make my--

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:45.622 align:middle line:84%
I should probably have made
this one lower everywhere.

00:26:45.622 --> 00:26:47.580 align:middle line:84%
Duopoly profits are
generally going to be lower

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:48.770 align:middle line:90%
than monopoly profits.

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:52.140 align:middle line:90%
So it might look like this.

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.840 align:middle line:84%
It may be that rather than go
all the way out here and drive

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:58.440 align:middle line:84%
my monopoly profits to
0, I'm better off just

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:01.260 align:middle line:84%
going somewhere, doing a
more moderate investment

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:04.170 align:middle line:84%
and admitting that firm 2
is going to be in the market

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:06.400 align:middle line:90%
and accommodate entry instead.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:11.130 align:middle line:84%
So if the profits from deterring
entry are sufficiently low,

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:14.280 align:middle line:84%
instead I may just go to
an accommodation strategy

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:17.970 align:middle line:84%
where I instead choose
k not to deter entry,

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.980 align:middle line:84%
but to do what's best
possible for given

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:21.455 align:middle line:90%
that firm 2 is going to enter.

00:27:21.455 --> 00:27:24.740 align:middle line:90%


00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So let me discuss the entry
accommodation solution.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:30.770 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to call that k1AC.

00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:36.410 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be the argmax of
overall k1 of pi 1 star of k1,

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:38.980 align:middle line:84%
where pi 1 star is the
duopoly profit function.

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:41.950 align:middle line:90%


00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:45.250 align:middle line:84%
So in this example,
let's suppose

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:48.690 align:middle line:84%
it looks like this,
it's maximized here.

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.710 align:middle line:90%
I could have k1 accommodation.

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:54.560 align:middle line:90%


00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:59.420 align:middle line:84%
I'm instead maximizing
the duopoly profits

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:02.515 align:middle line:84%
that I'm going to get if
I choose investment k1.

00:28:02.515 --> 00:28:08.470 align:middle line:90%


00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:11.800 align:middle line:84%
And so what Fudenberg
will do is give you

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:14.950 align:middle line:84%
a little bit of thinking
about, you know, when are you

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:18.070 align:middle line:84%
going to over-invest
or under-invest

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:20.120 align:middle line:90%
in order to accommodate entry?

00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:21.800 align:middle line:84%
How do you think
about that decision?

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:24.080 align:middle line:84%
I said the deterrence
one was simple.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:26.270 align:middle line:84%
You just think about I want
to hurt the other firm.

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:27.860 align:middle line:90%
If it's tough, I over-invest.

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:29.720 align:middle line:90%
If it's soft, I under-invest.

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:32.850 align:middle line:90%
How do we think about this?

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.030 align:middle line:90%
Well, so I need a benchmark.

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:39.670 align:middle line:84%
So as a point of comparison,
they define k1OL--

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:42.520 align:middle line:90%
OL stands for "open loop."

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:48.190 align:middle line:84%
That's a terminology that's
disappeared from economics

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:49.780 align:middle line:90%
since they wrote the paper.

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:53.830 align:middle line:84%
So we define k1 open loop to
be the level of investment

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:57.700 align:middle line:84%
that firm 1 would choose
if entry was going to occur

00:28:57.700 --> 00:29:01.340 align:middle line:90%
and firm 2 could not observe k1.

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:07.340 align:middle line:84%
So if firm 2 can't observe
k1, then in equilibrium it

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:09.440 align:middle line:84%
must be the case that
the partial of pi 1

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:13.160 align:middle line:84%
with respect to k1
evaluated at k1 open loop,

00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:17.810 align:middle line:84%
and then the equilibrium actions
that follow, is equal to 0.

00:29:17.810 --> 00:29:20.240 align:middle line:84%
OK, so think about the
equilibrium-- think

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:23.240 align:middle line:84%
about the game where
firm 1 chooses k1,

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:25.580 align:middle line:84%
and then firm 2
doesn't observe k1

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:27.440 align:middle line:84%
before it makes
its entry decision.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:29.750 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, it
correctly anticipates

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:34.280 align:middle line:84%
what the investment was, and
then the actions by both firms

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:37.760 align:middle line:90%
reflect what the investment was.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:42.390 align:middle line:84%
But so we get it's this
partial is equal to 0 evaluated

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:46.590 align:middle line:84%
at k1 open loop, because it's
almost as if firm 1 could

00:29:46.590 --> 00:29:50.010 align:middle line:84%
go back and change,
that k1 is not sunk,

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:52.830 align:middle line:84%
and firm 1 could go
back and change k1

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:55.998 align:middle line:84%
after firm 2 has
taken its action then,

00:29:55.998 --> 00:29:57.540 align:middle line:84%
because if firm 2
doesn't observe it,

00:29:57.540 --> 00:29:59.500 align:middle line:84%
it's as if you're
choosing it afterwards.

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:02.490 align:middle line:84%
It's the same first
order condition.

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:04.070 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:30:04.070 --> 00:30:05.400 align:middle line:90%
Is that definition OK?

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:12.442 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, we're going to think
about strategic investment

00:30:12.442 --> 00:30:14.650 align:middle line:84%
as being what do you do
different from what you would

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:16.042 align:middle line:90%
do if k1 was unobservable.

00:30:16.042 --> 00:30:17.500 align:middle line:84%
Because if k1 is
unobservable, it's

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:20.860 align:middle line:84%
not affecting the other
firm, the strategic use of it

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:24.310 align:middle line:84%
is doing something different
because firm 2 sees it.

00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:27.730 align:middle line:84%
And so first
characterization theorem

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:30.010 align:middle line:84%
just highlights the
strategic effect.

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:32.990 align:middle line:84%
Firms over-invest to
accommodate entry,

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:37.300 align:middle line:84%
meaning they choose k1 bigger
than the open loop value

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:44.620 align:middle line:84%
if and only if d pi 1 / da2, da2
star / dk1 is greater than 0.

00:30:44.620 --> 00:30:47.090 align:middle line:84%
OK, and the proof of
this is straightforward.

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:49.660 align:middle line:84%
I again, just write
out here what is-- now

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:54.130 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to write
out what is pi 1 star.

00:30:54.130 --> 00:30:55.420 align:middle line:90%
It's that.

00:30:55.420 --> 00:30:58.810 align:middle line:84%
And then think about what
is the derivative of pi 1

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:04.005 align:middle line:84%
star with respect to k1 when
I evaluate it at k1 open loop?

00:31:04.005 --> 00:31:06.520 align:middle line:90%


00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:11.560 align:middle line:84%
First you get a partial,
partial pi 1 with respect to--

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:15.140 align:middle line:84%
partial pi 1 with respect to
k1 evaluated at k1 open loop.

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:19.000 align:middle line:84%
That's 0 by the definition
of k1 open loop.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:23.530 align:middle line:84%
Second thing you get is d pi
1 / da1, da1 / star dk1 again

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:27.410 align:middle line:84%
with this d pi 1 / da1
evaluated at a1 star of k1.

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:30.910 align:middle line:84%
This is 0 by the
envelope theorem again.

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:37.660 align:middle line:84%
So d pi 1 star / dk1 is positive
if this strategic effect

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:40.570 align:middle line:84%
is positive, and it's negative
if the strategic effect is

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:41.420 align:middle line:90%
negative.

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:45.610 align:middle line:84%
So the strategic effect is how
does the investment affect a2,

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:50.230 align:middle line:84%
and how is your
profit affected by a2.

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:53.200 align:middle line:84%
Does k1 change the
other firm's action

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:55.160 align:middle line:84%
in a way that's
good or bad for you?

00:31:55.160 --> 00:31:56.960 align:middle line:90%
That's the strategic effect.

00:31:56.960 --> 00:32:00.670 align:middle line:84%
And so notice that if I
evaluate that derivative,

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:05.740 align:middle line:84%
if the total derivative of
pi 1 star with respect to k1

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:10.120 align:middle line:84%
evaluated at k1 open loop is
greater than 0 and profit is

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:16.960 align:middle line:84%
single peaked, then that's going
to imply that k1AC is strictly

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:18.940 align:middle line:90%
greater than k1 open loop.

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:23.560 align:middle line:84%
It's just a case where we
have a profit function we

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:25.760 align:middle line:90%
know evaluated at k1 open loop.

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:29.247 align:middle line:84%
This pi 1 star is
increasing, so the maximum

00:32:29.247 --> 00:32:30.330 align:middle line:90%
is somewhere to the right.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:34.660 align:middle line:90%


00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:37.060 align:middle line:84%
So one way to think about
strategic investment

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:39.590 align:middle line:84%
is just to do these
calculations for yourself.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:45.898 align:middle line:84%
How would this investment
affect firm 2's action?

00:32:45.898 --> 00:32:47.690 align:middle line:84%
And then what would I
think of that action?

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:53.440 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, cost reduction,
I'm in a price competition game,

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:57.190 align:middle line:84%
and I'm thinking of
investing in cost reduction.

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:00.030 align:middle line:84%
If I reduce my costs in
a price competition game,

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:02.530 align:middle line:84%
that's going to lead the other
firm to lower its equilibrium

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:05.180 align:middle line:84%
price because he knows I'm
going to lower my price.

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:08.860 align:middle line:84%
And so da2 / dk1 becomes
negative, more investment,

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:10.330 align:middle line:90%
lower rival price.

00:33:10.330 --> 00:33:13.220 align:middle line:84%
And then my profit is
increasing in the rival's price.

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:15.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is positive, so
positive times negative.

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:18.220 align:middle line:84%
This is a negative
strategic effect.

00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:22.270 align:middle line:84%
So in the strategic
entry accommodation game,

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:25.780 align:middle line:84%
you're going to underinvest
in cost reduction,

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:27.430 align:middle line:84%
meaning you're going
to do less of it

00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:31.055 align:middle line:84%
than you would do if you could
do secret cost reduction.

00:33:31.055 --> 00:33:32.680 align:middle line:84%
So if I could do
secret cost reduction,

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:34.700 align:middle line:84%
I would do more
because it really

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:38.070 align:middle line:84%
does benefit me profit
wise to have a lower cost.

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:40.730 align:middle line:84%
But I know that if my rival
sees me investing in the cost

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:42.880 align:middle line:84%
reduction, he's just
going to lower his price,

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:44.660 align:middle line:84%
so I'm going to eat up a lot
of the benefits I was getting

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:46.020 align:middle line:90%
from the cost reduction.

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:48.740 align:middle line:84%
So what we'll see
firms is underinvesting

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:49.875 align:middle line:90%
in cost reduction.

00:33:49.875 --> 00:33:52.250 align:middle line:84%
You could do a calculation
and say, why doesn't that firm

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:53.490 align:middle line:90%
adopt this technology?

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:56.880 align:middle line:84%
That technology would
clearly pay for itself.

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:59.330 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is they don't
do it for strategic reasons

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:01.910 align:middle line:84%
because if their rival
knew they had that lower

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:04.130 align:middle line:84%
cost manufacturing technology,
the rival would just

00:34:04.130 --> 00:34:05.040 align:middle line:90%
charge a lower price.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:05.873 align:middle line:90%
They'd be worse off.

00:34:05.873 --> 00:34:10.042 align:middle line:90%


00:34:10.042 --> 00:34:11.600 align:middle line:90%
Is that example pretty clear?

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:15.219 align:middle line:90%


00:34:15.219 --> 00:34:16.545 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:34:16.545 --> 00:34:18.670 align:middle line:84%
Fudenberg and Tirole give
the second theorem noting

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:23.590 align:middle line:84%
that one way to think about
strategic entry accommodation

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:28.060 align:middle line:84%
is in terms of, what's the
effect of the investment

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:28.850 align:middle line:90%
on your rival?

00:34:28.850 --> 00:34:30.110 align:middle line:90%
What is the rival?

00:34:30.110 --> 00:34:31.489 align:middle line:90%
What does that do to you?

00:34:31.489 --> 00:34:35.440 align:middle line:84%
They note that it also can be
related to the type of game

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:36.620 align:middle line:90%
and the type of investment.

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:38.030 align:middle line:90%
So here's a theorem.

00:34:38.030 --> 00:34:40.270 align:middle line:84%
Let's, for instance,
initially consider

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:43.300 align:middle line:84%
investments where there's
no direct effect on firm

00:34:43.300 --> 00:34:46.060 align:middle line:90%
2 like a cost reduction.

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:51.639 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that the game is at
least somewhat symmetric, weakly

00:34:51.639 --> 00:34:52.610 align:middle line:90%
symmetric, I'd call it.

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:55.960 align:middle line:84%
So profit-- changing
the sign of d pi i / daj

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:58.430 align:middle line:90%
is the same as d pi j / dai.

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:01.025 align:middle line:84%
So any game like price
competition or quantity

00:35:01.025 --> 00:35:02.650 align:middle line:84%
competition or whatever
will have this.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:04.990 align:middle line:84%
Just the way that
firm 1's action

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:06.610 align:middle line:84%
affects firm 2 is
the same as the way

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:09.100 align:middle line:90%
firm 2's action affects firm 1.

00:35:09.100 --> 00:35:11.110 align:middle line:84%
If you had some
bizarre game where

00:35:11.110 --> 00:35:14.720 align:middle line:84%
firm 1 was choosing a price and
firm 2 was choosing a quantity,

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:16.190 align:middle line:90%
then that might not be true.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:20.510 align:middle line:84%
But most of the games we
do are fully symmetric.

00:35:20.510 --> 00:35:25.040 align:middle line:84%
And so the theorem is that firms
overinvest to accommodate entry

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:28.340 align:middle line:84%
if the game has in strategic
complements and investment

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:32.480 align:middle line:84%
makes them soft,
or if the game has

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:36.500 align:middle line:84%
strategic substitutes and
investment makes firm 1 tough.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:43.130 align:middle line:84%
So let me start with the
strategic substitutes case.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:48.530 align:middle line:84%
In the strategic substitutes
case, what this says is--

00:35:48.530 --> 00:35:52.100 align:middle line:84%
so in strategic substitutes--
so I did put it over here.

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:58.430 align:middle line:84%
So in the substitutes case, if
the investment makes you tough,

00:35:58.430 --> 00:35:59.496 align:middle line:90%
you overinvest.

00:35:59.496 --> 00:36:02.391 align:middle line:90%


00:36:02.391 --> 00:36:05.016 align:middle line:84%
And if you have something that
makes you soft, you underinvest.

00:36:05.016 --> 00:36:07.560 align:middle line:90%


00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:11.610 align:middle line:84%
This is the same
thing we had in-- this

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:15.825 align:middle line:84%
is the same thing we had in
the entry deterrence case.

00:36:15.825 --> 00:36:17.880 align:middle line:84%
When you're in deter
entry, you're always

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:19.030 align:middle line:90%
trying to hurt your rivals.

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:21.130 align:middle line:84%
So if the investment makes
you tough, you do it.

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:23.110 align:middle line:84%
If it makes you soft,
you don't do it.

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:26.820 align:middle line:84%
What this says in games
with strategic substitutes,

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:30.510 align:middle line:84%
accommodation of entry is
just like deterrence of entry.

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:32.230 align:middle line:84%
In a game with
strategic substitutes,

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:36.440 align:middle line:84%
what you always want to do
is just hurt your rival.

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:42.950 align:middle line:84%
But things turn around in games
with strategic complements.

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:46.310 align:middle line:84%
In games with
strategic complements,

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:49.220 align:middle line:90%
you want to help your rival?

00:36:49.220 --> 00:36:51.470 align:middle line:84%
So actually, when I was
describing the cost reduction,

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:54.580 align:middle line:84%
I did the cost reduction here, I
said in the cost reduction game,

00:36:54.580 --> 00:36:56.410 align:middle line:84%
to deter entry, you
don't want to do it,

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:58.210 align:middle line:84%
but to accommodate
entry, you don't

00:36:58.210 --> 00:37:02.380 align:middle line:84%
invest because it's just going
to make your rival lower prices.

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:04.930 align:middle line:84%
But the interesting
observation here

00:37:04.930 --> 00:37:09.473 align:middle line:84%
is that sometimes I describe
Cournot versus Bertrand

00:37:09.473 --> 00:37:11.390 align:middle line:84%
as just two different
assumptions people make.

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:12.410 align:middle line:90%
They're similar models.

00:37:12.410 --> 00:37:14.020 align:middle line:90%
Profits decline.

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:16.362 align:middle line:84%
This notes that a lot
of the conclusions we're

00:37:16.362 --> 00:37:18.070 align:middle line:84%
going to make about
strategic investments

00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:21.820 align:middle line:84%
would just completely flip if
we had a quantity competition

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:24.730 align:middle line:84%
game versus if we had a
price competition game.

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:26.890 align:middle line:84%
So in the quantity
competition game--

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:31.000 align:middle line:84%
let me go back to my previous
first order condition.

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:34.930 align:middle line:84%
What's the strategic
effect of reducing my cost?

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:39.080 align:middle line:84%
In a quantity competition
game, if I reduce my cost,

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:42.130 align:middle line:84%
it's a commitment that I'm
going to produce a lot.

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:44.470 align:middle line:84%
And in Cournot with those
downward-sloping reaction

00:37:44.470 --> 00:37:46.180 align:middle line:84%
curves, if I'm going
to produce a lot,

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:48.310 align:middle line:84%
my rival is going
to produce less.

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:52.160 align:middle line:84%
And so by investing
in cost reduction,

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:54.380 align:middle line:90%
I make his quantity lower.

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:56.430 align:middle line:90%
So this is negative.

00:37:56.430 --> 00:38:01.120 align:middle line:84%
And then if his quantity is
higher, that's bad for me.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:02.200 align:middle line:90%
So this is also negative.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:03.575 align:middle line:84%
So I have negative
times negative

00:38:03.575 --> 00:38:07.890 align:middle line:84%
is a positive number, which
means that I want to overinvest

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.540 align:middle line:90%
in cost reduction.

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:13.200 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at firms that
would be competing in a Cournot

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.630 align:middle line:84%
market, the conclusion
you'd get is

00:38:15.630 --> 00:38:18.060 align:middle line:84%
that if they were considering
cost reduction-- if you look

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:20.640 align:middle line:84%
at price competing
firms, we're going

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:23.220 align:middle line:84%
to expect that there will be
these cost-reducing investments

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:25.345 align:middle line:84%
that we don't understand
why firm 1 isn't taking it

00:38:25.345 --> 00:38:27.000 align:middle line:84%
because it would be
privately optimal,

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:28.267 align:middle line:90%
holding everything else fixed.

00:38:28.267 --> 00:38:29.850 align:middle line:84%
In a quantity
competition game, you're

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:32.790 align:middle line:84%
going to see firms having
overinvested in cost reduction.

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:36.360 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to calculate
that that cost reduction

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:38.350 align:middle line:84%
technology is not
paying for itself.

00:38:38.350 --> 00:38:42.120 align:middle line:84%
It's making less money with
the cost-reducing technology

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:43.270 align:middle line:90%
than the amount.

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:45.130 align:middle line:84%
It's actually saving
on production.

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:48.060 align:middle line:84%
But it does it because
of the strategic effect.

00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:51.150 align:middle line:84%
That is, in a Cournot
game, committing

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:54.340 align:middle line:84%
to be tough forces your rival
to stay at a smaller scale.

00:38:54.340 --> 00:38:55.340 align:middle line:90%
And that's good for you.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:39:02.720 align:middle line:90%


00:39:02.720 --> 00:39:05.840 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I think
the thing is in games

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:09.500 align:middle line:84%
with strategic substitutes,
what you're always trying to do

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:11.920 align:middle line:84%
is take investments
that hurt your rival.

00:39:11.920 --> 00:39:14.750 align:middle line:90%


00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:18.440 align:middle line:84%
And what you're doing in a
game with strategic complements

00:39:18.440 --> 00:39:20.840 align:middle line:84%
is you're trying
to help your rival.

00:39:20.840 --> 00:39:23.795 align:middle line:84%
So anything you can do
that's good for your rival

00:39:23.795 --> 00:39:26.630 align:middle line:84%
is good for you as
well or at least

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:27.850 align:middle line:90%
that's the strategic effect.

00:39:27.850 --> 00:39:34.805 align:middle line:90%


00:39:34.805 --> 00:39:36.930 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just phrasing this
in the discussion of how

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:41.310 align:middle line:84%
does k1AC compare to k1OL, which
is the amount of investment you

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:46.570 align:middle line:84%
would be doing in the oligopoly
if it was unobservable.

00:39:46.570 --> 00:39:49.220 align:middle line:90%


00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:50.640 align:middle line:90%
Put a proof on the next slide.

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:53.120 align:middle line:84%
I think I won't take the
time to go through this

00:39:53.120 --> 00:39:56.430 align:middle line:84%
because sometimes
I find it doesn't--

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:57.790 align:middle line:90%
it's a simple calculation.

00:39:57.790 --> 00:39:59.623 align:middle line:84%
I don't know that it's
all that informative.

00:39:59.623 --> 00:40:01.265 align:middle line:84%
But basically, the
argument is just--

00:40:01.265 --> 00:40:03.940 align:middle line:90%


00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:07.380 align:middle line:90%
You know, think about how--

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:09.435 align:middle line:90%
think about how a1 star of k1--

00:40:09.435 --> 00:40:15.245 align:middle line:90%


00:40:15.245 --> 00:40:17.300 align:middle line:90%
what I'm trying to sign is this.

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:19.830 align:middle line:84%
I know this is the
strategic effect.

00:40:19.830 --> 00:40:24.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to overinvest if
k1 moves a2 in a direction

00:40:24.170 --> 00:40:25.250 align:middle line:90%
that I like.

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:27.710 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
underinvest if k1 moves a2

00:40:27.710 --> 00:40:29.430 align:middle line:90%
in a direction I don't like.

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:32.000 align:middle line:84%
So this was the
strategic effect.

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:35.300 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just trying to
think about how do I

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:36.950 align:middle line:90%
sign this strategic effect?

00:40:36.950 --> 00:40:41.510 align:middle line:84%
In particular, how do I find
out what da2 star / dk1 is?

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:44.840 align:middle line:84%
Is k1 going to move a2
in a direction I like

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:48.120 align:middle line:84%
or in the direction
I don't like?

00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:49.740 align:middle line:90%
And how do I do that?

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:54.280 align:middle line:84%
I note that a2 star
satisfies this for every k1.

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:56.760 align:middle line:84%
This is the first
order condition

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:59.940 align:middle line:84%
defining firm 2's
best response function

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:02.110 align:middle line:90%
or defining equilibrium.

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:06.030 align:middle line:84%
That partial of pi 2 with
respect to a2 evaluated

00:41:06.030 --> 00:41:08.710 align:middle line:90%
at a2 star of k1 is equal to 0.

00:41:08.710 --> 00:41:11.910 align:middle line:84%
So what I just do is
I take this thing.

00:41:11.910 --> 00:41:15.060 align:middle line:84%
I differentiate it
with respect to k1.

00:41:15.060 --> 00:41:17.760 align:middle line:90%
It gives me these three terms.

00:41:17.760 --> 00:41:22.770 align:middle line:84%
And then when I look
at this expression d pi

00:41:22.770 --> 00:41:26.080 align:middle line:84%
2 / dk1 was 0 with
no direct effect.

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:29.750 align:middle line:90%
So this term drops out.

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:33.390 align:middle line:84%
d squared pi 2 da1 /
da2, this is the thing

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:36.440 align:middle line:84%
that's whether you have
strategic complements

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:39.710 align:middle line:90%
or strategic substitutes.

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:42.030 align:middle line:90%
This term here is negative.

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:44.210 align:middle line:90%
And so anyway, you--

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:46.740 align:middle line:90%
I'm trying to sign da2 star dk1.

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:47.990 align:middle line:90%
I move this to the other side.

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:51.270 align:middle line:84%
And the sign of da2 star
/ dk1 is the sign of this.

00:41:51.270 --> 00:41:53.720 align:middle line:84%
And that depends on
complements or substitutes

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:58.390 align:middle line:90%
and the soft or tough.

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:05.110 align:middle line:90%


00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:07.450 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, you can look at the
calculations afterwards.

00:42:07.450 --> 00:42:10.540 align:middle line:90%
But in some sense, that's--

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:12.655 align:middle line:84%
the reason why is you're
just thinking about what

00:42:12.655 --> 00:42:14.290 align:middle line:90%
it is that's going to--

00:42:14.290 --> 00:42:16.540 align:middle line:84%
are you going to move the
other firm's action since it

00:42:16.540 --> 00:42:17.930 align:middle line:90%
hurts you or helps you?

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:19.420 align:middle line:84%
And that just
turns out to depend

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:22.600 align:middle line:90%
whether if it's complements--

00:42:22.600 --> 00:42:26.120 align:middle line:84%
like it's helping-- firm 2 is
helping you and vice versa.

00:42:26.120 --> 00:42:28.960 align:middle line:84%
And it substitutes-- helping
firm 2 is hurting you

00:42:28.960 --> 00:42:29.700 align:middle line:90%
and vice versa.

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:33.080 align:middle line:90%


00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:37.070 align:middle line:84%
But I think the main
thing to take away

00:42:37.070 --> 00:42:39.650 align:middle line:84%
is this intuition
for what you want

00:42:39.650 --> 00:42:42.740 align:middle line:84%
to do in entry accommodation
games is that in Cournot games,

00:42:42.740 --> 00:42:44.990 align:middle line:84%
you're always trying to still
hurt your rival like you

00:42:44.990 --> 00:42:46.610 align:middle line:90%
do to deter entry.

00:42:46.610 --> 00:42:49.250 align:middle line:84%
But in games with strategic
complements, things turn around,

00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:50.625 align:middle line:84%
and you should
always be thinking

00:42:50.625 --> 00:42:52.940 align:middle line:84%
about I want to aid my rival
and distort my investment

00:42:52.940 --> 00:42:54.400 align:middle line:90%
in that direction.

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:55.850 align:middle line:84%
And I just want
to do that quickly

00:42:55.850 --> 00:42:57.380 align:middle line:84%
because I'll just
go through now.

00:42:57.380 --> 00:43:00.920 align:middle line:84%
I've got like five different
examples of classic arguments

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:06.060 align:middle line:84%
to think about, what do firms
do for strategic reasons?

00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:09.300 align:middle line:84%
So the first is a model
of learning by doing.

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:14.370 align:middle line:84%
So we start with a firm
that's a monopolist.

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:18.050 align:middle line:90%
And it has some costs c0.

00:43:18.050 --> 00:43:22.220 align:middle line:84%
And it sells some quantity
in the first period, q11,

00:43:22.220 --> 00:43:26.390 align:middle line:84%
where I guess the upper
one refers to the--

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:27.170 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:43:27.170 --> 00:43:28.860 align:middle line:84%
I guess it refers
to the first period.

00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:31.940 align:middle line:84%
So it's firm 1 in
the first period.

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:35.690 align:middle line:84%
After firm 1 produced
in the first period,

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:39.080 align:middle line:84%
firm 2 observes firm
1's production level

00:43:39.080 --> 00:43:42.793 align:middle line:84%
and then chooses whether
to enter at a cost of E.

00:43:42.793 --> 00:43:44.960 align:middle line:84%
The critical assumption of
a learning by doing model

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:49.850 align:middle line:84%
is that the firm 1's marginal
cost in the second period

00:43:49.850 --> 00:43:53.540 align:middle line:84%
is going to depend on how much
it produced in the first period.

00:43:53.540 --> 00:43:58.200 align:middle line:84%
So c of q11 is the
period 2 marginal cost.

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:00.650 align:middle line:84%
And so we're assuming
that the more you produce

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:03.380 align:middle line:84%
in the first period, the
lower is your marginal cost

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:05.210 align:middle line:90%
in the second period.

00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:08.810 align:middle line:84%
So just by producing more, you
get more experience producing.

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:10.400 align:middle line:84%
Every unit you
produce, you figure out

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:12.600 align:middle line:84%
things that improve
your production process.

00:44:12.600 --> 00:44:16.410 align:middle line:90%
You make your costs lower.

00:44:16.410 --> 00:44:21.270 align:middle line:84%
So in this model, we can
think of overproducing.

00:44:21.270 --> 00:44:24.780 align:middle line:90%
Overproducing is just choosing--

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:28.020 align:middle line:84%
I would normally choose q11
if I was-- if there was--

00:44:28.020 --> 00:44:32.760 align:middle line:84%
in the blockaded entry case,
I would choose q11 to be

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:45.330 align:middle line:84%
the argmax over
q11 of pi 1 of q11,

00:44:45.330 --> 00:44:48.340 align:middle line:90%
pi 11 plus discount factor--

00:44:48.340 --> 00:44:50.070 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I do
discount factors--

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:57.540 align:middle line:90%
a pi 12 monopoly of q11 q12.

00:44:57.540 --> 00:45:00.570 align:middle line:84%
So my first period profit
just depends on the quantity

00:45:00.570 --> 00:45:02.280 align:middle line:90%
I produce in the first period.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:04.680 align:middle line:84%
And in the second period,
if I'm still a monopolist,

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:06.480 align:middle line:84%
my profit is going to
depend both on what

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:08.867 align:middle line:84%
I produced in the
first period and what

00:45:08.867 --> 00:45:10.950 align:middle line:84%
I produced in the second
period with the first one

00:45:10.950 --> 00:45:13.750 align:middle line:84%
here because what I
produce in the first period

00:45:13.750 --> 00:45:16.630 align:middle line:84%
affects my cost in
the second period.

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:20.830 align:middle line:90%
And so this is what I would do.

00:45:20.830 --> 00:45:23.060 align:middle line:84%
I think I called
this q1 monopoly.

00:45:23.060 --> 00:45:27.070 align:middle line:84%
This is what you would do in
the blockaded entry case is just

00:45:27.070 --> 00:45:29.780 align:middle line:90%
produce this level of output.

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:34.840 align:middle line:84%
And so you can think of if I
choose q11 greater than q1m,

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:41.020 align:middle line:84%
that's an investment
in reducing costs.

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:45.470 align:middle line:90%


00:45:45.470 --> 00:45:47.080 align:middle line:84%
It's an investment
in reducing costs

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:49.060 align:middle line:84%
that I make by
just overproducing

00:45:49.060 --> 00:45:50.630 align:middle line:90%
in the first period.

00:45:50.630 --> 00:45:52.580 align:middle line:84%
What's the cost
of the investment?

00:45:52.580 --> 00:46:03.490 align:middle line:84%
The cost of the investment is
pi 11 of q1m minus pi 11 of q11.

00:46:03.490 --> 00:46:05.620 align:middle line:90%
If I produce this--

00:46:05.620 --> 00:46:08.620 align:middle line:84%
if I produce some
extra amount, it's

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:10.640 align:middle line:84%
going to lower my
first period profits.

00:46:10.640 --> 00:46:13.240 align:middle line:90%
The amount by which it lower--

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:15.600 align:middle line:84%
so this is in some sense
the investment cost.

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:21.190 align:middle line:90%


00:46:21.190 --> 00:46:23.380 align:middle line:84%
So producing much
in the first period

00:46:23.380 --> 00:46:25.420 align:middle line:84%
is a strategic
investment, an investment

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:27.820 align:middle line:84%
that I spend something
and lost profits.

00:46:27.820 --> 00:46:30.360 align:middle line:84%
I gain something in having
reduced my future costs.

00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:33.220 align:middle line:90%


00:46:33.220 --> 00:46:38.000 align:middle line:90%
So-- oh, I put a B on here.

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:40.750 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose that
there's no firm two.

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:45.610 align:middle line:84%
Then the blockaded entry
level would be q1B.

00:46:45.610 --> 00:46:48.682 align:middle line:84%
If I need to deter entry,
and I want to deter entry,

00:46:48.682 --> 00:46:50.890 align:middle line:84%
then what I'm going to do
is I'm going to overproduce

00:46:50.890 --> 00:46:53.140 align:middle line:90%
because investment makes--

00:46:53.140 --> 00:46:54.430 align:middle line:90%
it reduces my cost.

00:46:54.430 --> 00:46:55.250 align:middle line:90%
It makes me tough.

00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:57.440 align:middle line:84%
I want to hurt firm two,
so I'm going to choose.

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:58.810 align:middle line:90%
q11 greater than q1B.

00:46:58.810 --> 00:47:02.270 align:middle line:90%


00:47:02.270 --> 00:47:06.890 align:middle line:90%
In the entry accommodation case.

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:10.130 align:middle line:84%
I'm either going to want to
underproduce or overproduce,

00:47:10.130 --> 00:47:12.680 align:middle line:84%
depending on whether I'm
playing a price competition game

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:14.600 align:middle line:90%
or a quantity competition game.

00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:16.820 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm playing a
price competition game

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:18.440 align:middle line:84%
where now suddenly
I want to be nice,

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:21.740 align:middle line:84%
in price competition games,
I'm going to distort downward--

00:47:21.740 --> 00:47:25.280 align:middle line:90%
have q11 less than a1 open loop.

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:28.040 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
distort downward

00:47:28.040 --> 00:47:29.987 align:middle line:84%
because I want to
keep my costs high,

00:47:29.987 --> 00:47:32.570 align:middle line:84%
and I want firm two to know that
I'm going to have high costs,

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:34.127 align:middle line:84%
that firm two,
when it enters, is

00:47:34.127 --> 00:47:35.960 align:middle line:84%
going to also charge a
high price because it

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:37.500 align:middle line:90%
knows I have high costs.

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:39.900 align:middle line:84%
So in the accommodation
game, I want to be nice.

00:47:39.900 --> 00:47:42.690 align:middle line:84%
I do that by underinvesting
in cost reduction.

00:47:42.690 --> 00:47:47.510 align:middle line:84%
Note, also, that we're
going to typically have--

00:47:47.510 --> 00:47:50.600 align:middle line:84%
q1 open loop is
going to typically

00:47:50.600 --> 00:47:52.880 align:middle line:90%
be less than q1 blockaded.

00:47:52.880 --> 00:48:00.950 align:middle line:90%


00:48:00.950 --> 00:48:03.540 align:middle line:84%
So to deter entry, I'm
going to produce more than I

00:48:03.540 --> 00:48:05.820 align:middle line:90%
get with blockaded entry.

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:08.250 align:middle line:84%
And then to accommodate,
I'm going to produce less

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:10.590 align:middle line:90%
than the open loop value.

00:48:10.590 --> 00:48:14.770 align:middle line:84%
And why do I say the open
loop value is typically

00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:17.470 align:middle line:84%
going to be less than
the blockaded value?

00:48:17.470 --> 00:48:22.420 align:middle line:84%
When I-- in the first period,
I'm investing in cost reduction.

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:25.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm investing in cost
reduction in part

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:27.670 align:middle line:84%
because I'm then going to have
lower cost in every unit I

00:48:27.670 --> 00:48:30.760 align:middle line:90%
sell in the second period.

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:33.470 align:middle line:84%
In the blockaded case, that's
what I'm taking into account.

00:48:33.470 --> 00:48:35.762 align:middle line:84%
How many units am I going to
sell in the second period?

00:48:35.762 --> 00:48:37.240 align:middle line:84%
What's my cost
savings going to be?

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:38.980 align:middle line:84%
In the open loop case,
it's going to be--

00:48:38.980 --> 00:48:40.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be in a duopoly
in the second period,

00:48:40.870 --> 00:48:42.885 align:middle line:84%
so I'm only going to be
serving half the market.

00:48:42.885 --> 00:48:44.260 align:middle line:84%
When I'm serving
half the market,

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:46.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to sell fewer
units in the second period.

00:48:46.700 --> 00:48:50.060 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, I invest less in cost
reduction in the first period.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:54.940 align:middle line:84%
So I do less overproduction
relative to what maximizes this

00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:58.030 align:middle line:84%
because I know that
I'm only going to be--

00:48:58.030 --> 00:49:00.488 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be selling fewer
units of t equals 2 anyway.

00:49:00.488 --> 00:49:02.030 align:middle line:84%
I don't value cost
reduction as much.

00:49:02.030 --> 00:49:04.770 align:middle line:84%
I go closer to just the
static monopoly profit.

00:49:04.770 --> 00:49:10.520 align:middle line:90%


00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:14.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we were playing in a
Cournot game, in a Cournot game,

00:49:14.600 --> 00:49:18.800 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to choose q11
greater than a1 open loop.

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:22.370 align:middle line:84%
So this would be
in a Bertrand kind

00:49:22.370 --> 00:49:25.670 align:middle line:90%
of game or a Hotelling game.

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:30.795 align:middle line:84%
In a Cournot game, what I'm
going to know is that Cournot--

00:49:30.795 --> 00:49:34.550 align:middle line:90%


00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:39.800 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to know that q1 open
loop is less than a1 blockaded

00:49:39.800 --> 00:49:42.980 align:middle line:90%
less than q1 entry deterrence.

00:49:42.980 --> 00:49:48.110 align:middle line:90%
And then q1 accommodation--

00:49:48.110 --> 00:49:49.610 align:middle line:84%
I don't know where
it's going to be.

00:49:49.610 --> 00:49:52.490 align:middle line:90%
It could be here.

00:49:52.490 --> 00:49:54.150 align:middle line:90%
It could be here.

00:49:54.150 --> 00:49:57.860 align:middle line:84%
We know it's bigger
than q1 open loop.

00:49:57.860 --> 00:50:00.960 align:middle line:84%
But q1 loop is less
than q1 blockaded.

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:03.860 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that you
overinvest, but still not as

00:50:03.860 --> 00:50:05.460 align:middle line:90%
much as you would do here.

00:50:05.460 --> 00:50:07.335 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be that you
have to produce a ton

00:50:07.335 --> 00:50:09.752 align:middle line:84%
to keep the other firm out,
but keeping the other firm out

00:50:09.752 --> 00:50:10.830 align:middle line:90%
is really important.

00:50:10.830 --> 00:50:13.700 align:middle line:84%
It could be you're producing
up here, or even more--

00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:17.570 align:middle line:90%
even up here or something.

00:50:17.570 --> 00:50:18.440 align:middle line:90%
I guess this is--

00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:19.523 align:middle line:90%
it's you're investing to--

00:50:19.523 --> 00:50:21.800 align:middle line:90%


00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:22.880 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:50:22.880 --> 00:50:25.250 align:middle line:84%
Yeah. you're investing
just to reduce his profits

00:50:25.250 --> 00:50:28.760 align:middle line:90%
to reduce his output, I guess.

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:31.320 align:middle line:84%
This is reducing his
output all the way to zero.

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:33.512 align:middle line:84%
So if you're reducing his
output all the way to zero

00:50:33.512 --> 00:50:35.220 align:middle line:84%
would be the biggest
benefit you can get.

00:50:35.220 --> 00:50:37.345 align:middle line:84%
So this one would have a
smaller benefit than this.

00:50:37.345 --> 00:50:41.540 align:middle line:84%
So I guess it would be
either here or here.

00:50:41.540 --> 00:50:45.200 align:middle line:84%
But in the competition game,
I'm just overinvesting,

00:50:45.200 --> 00:50:47.300 align:middle line:84%
but I'm overinvesting,
recognizing that it's still

00:50:47.300 --> 00:50:48.217 align:middle line:90%
going to be a duopoly.

00:50:48.217 --> 00:50:51.190 align:middle line:84%
So I'm overinvesting relative
to the somewhat lower level.

00:50:51.190 --> 00:50:57.600 align:middle line:90%


00:50:57.600 --> 00:51:00.810 align:middle line:84%
Leverage theory-- so there's
this old classic literature

00:51:00.810 --> 00:51:05.970 align:middle line:84%
in IO about, if you're a
firm, and you're a monopolist,

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:07.297 align:middle line:90%
do you get one monopoly?

00:51:07.297 --> 00:51:09.630 align:middle line:84%
It's like-- there's a big
Chicago school of thought that

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:11.940 align:middle line:84%
says if you have
one monopoly, you

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:14.680 align:middle line:84%
can only earn one
monopoly profit from it.

00:51:14.680 --> 00:51:16.940 align:middle line:90%
And so if you can only get one--

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:18.930 align:middle line:90%
you would never do something--

00:51:18.930 --> 00:51:21.570 align:middle line:84%
you would never use your
monopoly power in one market

00:51:21.570 --> 00:51:23.745 align:middle line:84%
to try to get extra
profits in a second market.

00:51:23.745 --> 00:51:25.620 align:middle line:84%
Because if you're trying
to use your monopoly

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:27.078 align:middle line:84%
to get profit in
the second market,

00:51:27.078 --> 00:51:28.380 align:middle line:90%
the only way you can do that--

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:30.297 align:middle line:84%
Like, if all I can do
is sell people something

00:51:30.297 --> 00:51:31.920 align:middle line:90%
they don't like as much--

00:51:31.920 --> 00:51:32.790 align:middle line:90%
like, if I'm Apple.

00:51:32.790 --> 00:51:36.150 align:middle line:84%
I try to monopolize the
iPhone charger market.

00:51:36.150 --> 00:51:37.830 align:middle line:84%
People don't like
iPhone chargers

00:51:37.830 --> 00:51:41.120 align:middle line:84%
as much as they would
have liked USB C chargers.

00:51:41.120 --> 00:51:43.500 align:middle line:84%
Yes, I can charge them
more by making them all

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:44.560 align:middle line:90%
buy chargers for me.

00:51:44.560 --> 00:51:46.283 align:middle line:90%
But anything that they--

00:51:46.283 --> 00:51:48.200 align:middle line:84%
anything that I'm making
selling the chargers,

00:51:48.200 --> 00:51:49.930 align:middle line:84%
I could have just charged
them more for the phone

00:51:49.930 --> 00:51:52.138 align:middle line:84%
to begin with and let them
buy the charger they want.

00:51:52.138 --> 00:51:53.440 align:middle line:90%
So all I'm doing is just--

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:55.390 align:middle line:84%
I'm just getting
one monopoly profit.

00:51:55.390 --> 00:51:57.190 align:middle line:84%
And you can't extend
your monopoly power

00:51:57.190 --> 00:51:59.572 align:middle line:84%
to a second market because
anything people pay extra

00:51:59.572 --> 00:52:01.030 align:middle line:84%
in the second
market, they're going

00:52:01.030 --> 00:52:03.718 align:middle line:84%
to treat as a cost of
buying your initial product.

00:52:03.718 --> 00:52:06.010 align:middle line:84%
And they're just going to
pay less for the base product

00:52:06.010 --> 00:52:08.690 align:middle line:84%
because you've tried to make
them buy a second product.

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:14.340 align:middle line:84%
So that's the Chicago
school critique that says,

00:52:14.340 --> 00:52:16.680 align:middle line:84%
no, you can't tie
two markets together

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:18.940 align:middle line:90%
and get any more profits.

00:52:18.940 --> 00:52:20.910 align:middle line:90%
And then so--

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:24.960 align:middle line:84%
Mike Whinston has this paper,
classic paper, saying sometimes

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:28.210 align:middle line:84%
you will want to try to extend
profits to a second market.

00:52:28.210 --> 00:52:31.068 align:middle line:84%
And this comes from a
strategic investment--

00:52:31.068 --> 00:52:33.360 align:middle line:84%
either strategic entry
accommodation or strategic entry

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:35.070 align:middle line:90%
deterrence motive.

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:38.610 align:middle line:84%
So suppose you have
a firm, firm one.

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:42.390 align:middle line:84%
It's a monopolist in good A.
And suppose that there's some--

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:46.930 align:middle line:84%
suppose that Samsung and
whatever has disappeared.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:49.380 align:middle line:90%
So now there's only iPhones.

00:52:49.380 --> 00:52:51.990 align:middle line:84%
So firm one is an
iPhone monopolist,

00:52:51.990 --> 00:52:53.940 align:middle line:84%
but it does compete
with firm two

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:58.050 align:middle line:84%
in producing chargers or
earbuds or something like that.

00:52:58.050 --> 00:53:01.720 align:middle line:84%
Suppose we have a homogeneous--
simple, homogeneous model.

00:53:01.720 --> 00:53:03.630 align:middle line:84%
All consumers get
value v from good

00:53:03.630 --> 00:53:06.930 align:middle line:84%
And Then there's a
demand for good B

00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:16.340 align:middle line:84%
that depends on prices for good
one and prices for good two.

00:53:16.340 --> 00:53:18.417 align:middle line:90%


00:53:18.417 --> 00:53:20.000 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just thinking,
in this example,

00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:21.417 align:middle line:84%
this has to be
something where you

00:53:21.417 --> 00:53:24.980 align:middle line:84%
can use good B without good
A. So I guess the phone

00:53:24.980 --> 00:53:26.800 align:middle line:84%
and the charger
does not work here.

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:33.020 align:middle line:90%


00:53:33.020 --> 00:53:33.520 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:35.680 align:middle line:90%
So suppose these are--

00:53:35.680 --> 00:53:39.200 align:middle line:84%
this is taking us
back in the day.

00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:41.410 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that Apple's
the iPhone monopolist.

00:53:41.410 --> 00:53:43.127 align:middle line:90%
Good B is headphones.

00:53:43.127 --> 00:53:44.710 align:middle line:84%
But suppose that
headphones could also

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:47.350 align:middle line:84%
be used to listen to
an MP3 player, which

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:49.000 align:middle line:84%
was a separate
object that existed

00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:51.040 align:middle line:84%
that you could use to
listen to music on, not

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:52.090 align:middle line:90%
through your phone.

00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:54.067 align:middle line:84%
So you could just
have a separate thing

00:53:54.067 --> 00:53:56.400 align:middle line:84%
that looked like a phone that
you carried in your pocket

00:53:56.400 --> 00:54:00.340 align:middle line:84%
that you could play music on
and listen to music on it.

00:54:00.340 --> 00:54:00.840 align:middle line:90%
So anyway.

00:54:00.840 --> 00:54:02.800 align:middle line:84%
So there's a demand
for headphones

00:54:02.800 --> 00:54:05.748 align:middle line:84%
even if you don't have a-- even
if you can't afford a phone,

00:54:05.748 --> 00:54:07.290 align:middle line:84%
you just have
headphones that you use

00:54:07.290 --> 00:54:09.780 align:middle line:90%
to listen to music otherwise.

00:54:09.780 --> 00:54:10.350 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:54:10.350 --> 00:54:12.040 align:middle line:90%
So firm one has this choice.

00:54:12.040 --> 00:54:14.515 align:middle line:84%
It can design its products
so that they cannot be used

00:54:14.515 --> 00:54:17.140 align:middle line:84%
separately, or it can design it
so that you just plug and play.

00:54:17.140 --> 00:54:20.130 align:middle line:84%
You can use anybody's
earphones with your iPhone.

00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:23.380 align:middle line:84%
So imagine first firm one
makes a design decision.

00:54:23.380 --> 00:54:25.980 align:middle line:84%
Then firm two chooses
whether to enter and compete

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:26.890 align:middle line:90%
with the monopolist.

00:54:26.890 --> 00:54:28.765 align:middle line:84%
And then we either get
a monopoly or duopoly.

00:54:28.765 --> 00:54:31.730 align:middle line:90%


00:54:31.730 --> 00:54:37.940 align:middle line:84%
In the-- if firm two comes in,
it's going to set a price for--

00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:39.860 align:middle line:84%
firm two is going to
set a price for good B

00:54:39.860 --> 00:54:42.140 align:middle line:84%
and doesn't sell
good A. And then

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:46.280 align:middle line:84%
firm one is either
going to be only selling

00:54:46.280 --> 00:54:49.880 align:middle line:84%
a bundle of a
phone with earbuds,

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:52.530 align:middle line:84%
or it's going to be selling
the two products separately.

00:54:52.530 --> 00:54:53.938 align:middle line:90%
You can buy the phone from it.

00:54:53.938 --> 00:54:55.730 align:middle line:84%
You can buy the earbuds
from it separately,

00:54:55.730 --> 00:54:59.090 align:middle line:84%
but they're sold at
two separate prices.

00:54:59.090 --> 00:55:01.880 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to think
about in this model,

00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:06.020 align:middle line:84%
would Apple design its product
so that every phone comes

00:55:06.020 --> 00:55:09.230 align:middle line:84%
with earbuds included, so
that no one would then ever

00:55:09.230 --> 00:55:11.080 align:middle line:90%
buy earphones from anyone else?

00:55:11.080 --> 00:55:13.860 align:middle line:90%


00:55:13.860 --> 00:55:20.370 align:middle line:84%
So absent any strategic effects,
if you can't prevent entry,

00:55:20.370 --> 00:55:23.610 align:middle line:84%
you can't alter p2,
firm one would never

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:25.650 align:middle line:90%
tie the products together.

00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:30.990 align:middle line:84%
Because if you sell this
bundle at price p1 AB,

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:35.370 align:middle line:84%
you could instead just sell
good A to everyone at price v,

00:55:35.370 --> 00:55:39.980 align:middle line:84%
and then sell the bundle
at price p1 AB minus v.

00:55:39.980 --> 00:55:45.750 align:middle line:90%
And if you do this, every--

00:55:45.750 --> 00:55:48.420 align:middle line:84%
once-- with this separate
pricing strategy,

00:55:48.420 --> 00:55:50.550 align:middle line:84%
everybody in the world is
going to buy the iPhone

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:58.300 align:middle line:84%
at price v. And sales
of good B are unchanged.

00:55:58.300 --> 00:56:02.240 align:middle line:84%
Because anybody who-- people
are making this choice.

00:56:02.240 --> 00:56:03.340 align:middle line:90%
Do I buy the Apple bundle?

00:56:03.340 --> 00:56:06.610 align:middle line:84%
Or do I just not have a
phone and just buy earphones

00:56:06.610 --> 00:56:08.440 align:middle line:90%
to listen to my MP3 player?

00:56:08.440 --> 00:56:12.010 align:middle line:84%
Anybody-- you sell
exactly the same--

00:56:12.010 --> 00:56:14.830 align:middle line:90%
anyone who values the iPhone--

00:56:14.830 --> 00:56:19.780 align:middle line:84%
anyone who values the Apple
earbuds at more than this

00:56:19.780 --> 00:56:21.110 align:middle line:90%
is going to buy from Apple.

00:56:21.110 --> 00:56:23.500 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who buys from less
than this is going to now--

00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:25.400 align:middle line:84%
is going to buy
it not from Apple.

00:56:25.400 --> 00:56:27.910 align:middle line:84%
And so you sell
exactly the same number

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:31.730 align:middle line:84%
of units of earphones at
these unbundled prices.

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:34.150 align:middle line:84%
But you sell more phones because
there are some people who

00:56:34.150 --> 00:56:37.512 align:middle line:84%
would like the phone
as a standalone,

00:56:37.512 --> 00:56:39.220 align:middle line:84%
but they really like
the outside earbuds,

00:56:39.220 --> 00:56:41.803 align:middle line:84%
so that they buy-- they don't
buy the phone when they can only

00:56:41.803 --> 00:56:45.440 align:middle line:84%
buy the phone bundled
with the earbuds.

00:56:45.440 --> 00:56:49.323 align:middle line:84%
So in a nonstrategic
model, you would never

00:56:49.323 --> 00:56:51.740 align:middle line:84%
tie these two products together
because it's always better

00:56:51.740 --> 00:56:53.448 align:middle line:84%
to let people buy what
they want and just

00:56:53.448 --> 00:56:55.050 align:middle line:84%
have the two of them
sold separately.

00:56:55.050 --> 00:56:57.710 align:middle line:90%


00:56:57.710 --> 00:57:00.710 align:middle line:84%
But think about
tying the product--

00:57:00.710 --> 00:57:03.170 align:middle line:84%
tying the product-- it's
a strategic investment

00:57:03.170 --> 00:57:04.760 align:middle line:90%
that makes firm one tough.

00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:07.250 align:middle line:90%
It hurts firms two.

00:57:07.250 --> 00:57:08.250 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:10.970 align:middle line:90%
Well, because it's as if--

00:57:10.970 --> 00:57:13.580 align:middle line:84%
you've got this profit
you're earning on good A--

00:57:13.580 --> 00:57:15.620 align:middle line:90%
v minus CA.

00:57:15.620 --> 00:57:21.380 align:middle line:84%
But you earn that profit on
good A only if you sell good B.

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:24.920 align:middle line:84%
So it's as if your
opportunity cost of selling B

00:57:24.920 --> 00:57:27.290 align:middle line:84%
is reduced from the
cost of producing

00:57:27.290 --> 00:57:32.280 align:middle line:84%
good B to the cost of good B
minus this opportunity cost.

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:35.490 align:middle line:84%
So every time I sell good
B, I'm selling good A--

00:57:35.490 --> 00:57:37.520 align:middle line:84%
every time I sell good
B, I'm selling good A

00:57:37.520 --> 00:57:38.740 align:middle line:90%
and earning this profit.

00:57:38.740 --> 00:57:40.980 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, this is
my opportunity-- that's

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:43.910 align:middle line:84%
my actual cost of
selling good B.

00:57:43.910 --> 00:57:49.340 align:middle line:84%
And so this is like
bundling the goods together.

00:57:49.340 --> 00:57:51.030 align:middle line:90%
You can think of it as a cost.

00:57:51.030 --> 00:57:55.540 align:middle line:84%
It's like a cost reduction
on good B. Or it's similar--

00:57:55.540 --> 00:57:59.370 align:middle line:84%
it's identical to a cost
reduction algebraically.

00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:01.620 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, if
you want to tie--

00:58:01.620 --> 00:58:07.470 align:middle line:84%
if you want to prevent any
firm from entering market B,

00:58:07.470 --> 00:58:10.470 align:middle line:84%
I tie the goods
together to deter entry.

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:12.240 align:middle line:84%
Because tying the
goods together is

00:58:12.240 --> 00:58:15.400 align:middle line:84%
going to reduce the number of
headphones that can get sold.

00:58:15.400 --> 00:58:18.570 align:middle line:84%
And by reducing the number of
headphones that can get sold,

00:58:18.570 --> 00:58:20.190 align:middle line:84%
I may keep firm two
out of the market

00:58:20.190 --> 00:58:22.920 align:middle line:84%
entirely and have a monopoly
on the phone-- on both of them

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:23.880 align:middle line:90%
together.

00:58:23.880 --> 00:58:28.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is a story where if you
think about the entry decision,

00:58:28.350 --> 00:58:30.895 align:middle line:84%
you do have an incentive
to tie goods together.

00:58:30.895 --> 00:58:32.520 align:middle line:84%
And by tying the
goods together, you're

00:58:32.520 --> 00:58:33.690 align:middle line:84%
earning higher
profits than you would

00:58:33.690 --> 00:58:34.930 align:middle line:90%
earn not tying them together.

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:37.860 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, you're going
beyond your monopoly profits

00:58:37.860 --> 00:58:40.790 align:middle line:84%
in firm one and also gaining
profits on market two.

00:58:40.790 --> 00:58:44.100 align:middle line:90%


00:58:44.100 --> 00:58:47.687 align:middle line:84%
In the entry
accommodation game, this

00:58:47.687 --> 00:58:49.270 align:middle line:84%
is an investment
that makes you tough.

00:58:49.270 --> 00:58:52.980 align:middle line:84%
So if you're in price
competition in market B,

00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:57.970 align:middle line:84%
you would not want to make this
tie because it hurts firm two.

00:58:57.970 --> 00:59:01.050 align:middle line:84%
And in a price competition game,
you want to help your rivals.

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:04.590 align:middle line:84%
So if you are competing in a
price competition game in market

00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:06.810 align:middle line:84%
B, then in the
entry accommodate--

00:59:06.810 --> 00:59:09.400 align:middle line:84%
then you would not want to
tie the products together.

00:59:09.400 --> 00:59:12.580 align:middle line:84%
And the leverage theory of
tying doesn't make any sense.

00:59:12.580 --> 00:59:17.190 align:middle line:84%
You can't be tying them in
order to get extra profits.

00:59:17.190 --> 00:59:20.260 align:middle line:90%
You would always only tie--

00:59:20.260 --> 00:59:22.260 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, you're
going to have an incentive

00:59:22.260 --> 00:59:24.540 align:middle line:84%
to not tie them
because not tying them

00:59:24.540 --> 00:59:27.270 align:middle line:84%
strategically gets firm
two to price higher.

00:59:27.270 --> 00:59:30.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, if somehow this was an
example where you had a Cournot

00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:34.890 align:middle line:84%
market for good B, if good B was
being sold in a Cournot market,

00:59:34.890 --> 00:59:40.350 align:middle line:84%
then you may want to also
tie products in order--

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:42.735 align:middle line:84%
so it's like, I'm doing
something or other.

00:59:42.735 --> 00:59:44.110 align:middle line:84%
If you buy this
from me, you also

00:59:44.110 --> 00:59:46.010 align:middle line:90%
have to buy your coal from me.

00:59:46.010 --> 00:59:48.500 align:middle line:84%
And then coal was produced
in a Cournot market,

00:59:48.500 --> 00:59:51.760 align:middle line:84%
I might want to tie
goods together in order--

00:59:51.760 --> 00:59:55.180 align:middle line:84%
for the same reason, to be mean
to you to get you to produce

00:59:55.180 --> 00:59:57.030 align:middle line:90%
less coal so that we're--

00:59:57.030 --> 00:59:59.800 align:middle line:90%


00:59:59.800 --> 01:00:02.550 align:middle line:84%
that I'm hurting you in
the accommodation game.

01:00:02.550 --> 01:00:08.770 align:middle line:90%


01:00:08.770 --> 01:00:13.150 align:middle line:84%
But I think it does suggest
that it's the leverage

01:00:13.150 --> 01:00:16.030 align:middle line:84%
to deter entry is an
easy story to tell,

01:00:16.030 --> 01:00:18.070 align:middle line:84%
and leverage to
accommodate entry,

01:00:18.070 --> 01:00:20.720 align:middle line:84%
or the tying to
accommodate entry,

01:00:20.720 --> 01:00:23.820 align:middle line:84%
you're going to have to come
up with strategic substitutes.

01:00:23.820 --> 01:00:25.570 align:middle line:84%
I think it's a strategic
substitutes game,

01:00:25.570 --> 01:00:26.970 align:middle line:90%
and that's going to be--

01:00:26.970 --> 01:00:28.905 align:middle line:84%
not apply to many
things that we think of.

01:00:28.905 --> 01:00:31.470 align:middle line:90%


01:00:31.470 --> 01:00:33.480 align:middle line:90%
So limit pricing.

01:00:33.480 --> 01:00:37.410 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, the next two stories
I'm going to say is--

01:00:37.410 --> 01:00:39.210 align:middle line:84%
again, as I've said,
many things can

01:00:39.210 --> 01:00:42.640 align:middle line:84%
be thought of as an investment
in these previous stories.

01:00:42.640 --> 01:00:44.860 align:middle line:84%
An investment is
anything that's sunk.

01:00:44.860 --> 01:00:47.713 align:middle line:84%
And once it's sunk, you
can't easily undo it.

01:00:47.713 --> 01:00:49.380 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to
affect the competition

01:00:49.380 --> 01:00:51.870 align:middle line:90%
after the rival enters.

01:00:51.870 --> 01:00:55.660 align:middle line:84%
Can we think of prices as
an entry-deterring device?

01:00:55.660 --> 01:00:57.510 align:middle line:84%
So the classic idea
of limit pricing

01:00:57.510 --> 01:01:00.720 align:middle line:84%
is that you have a
firm facing entry.

01:01:00.720 --> 01:01:03.340 align:middle line:84%
And the thought in the limit
pricing literature was that way.

01:01:03.340 --> 01:01:04.923 align:middle line:84%
You had this idea
that firms are going

01:01:04.923 --> 01:01:06.780 align:middle line:84%
to set low prices
because they want

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:09.550 align:middle line:84%
to keep rivals from entering
and competing with them.

01:01:09.550 --> 01:01:12.513 align:middle line:84%
And there's a natural critique
of that idea, this limit pricing

01:01:12.513 --> 01:01:13.680 align:middle line:90%
idea, that says, well, wait.

01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:14.590 align:middle line:90%
How would that work?

01:01:14.590 --> 01:01:16.673 align:middle line:84%
Sure, you can set your
prices low so that everyone

01:01:16.673 --> 01:01:18.060 align:middle line:90%
sees that your prices are low.

01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:19.858 align:middle line:84%
But they know that
if you enter, we're

01:01:19.858 --> 01:01:22.150 align:middle line:84%
just going to play the static
Nash equilibrium anyways.

01:01:22.150 --> 01:01:25.260 align:middle line:84%
The fact that you wasted money
in all the previous periods

01:01:25.260 --> 01:01:29.820 align:middle line:84%
by pricing below the
monopoly price, it's just--

01:01:29.820 --> 01:01:33.190 align:middle line:84%
it's a sunk loss,
but it just-- it has

01:01:33.190 --> 01:01:34.440 align:middle line:90%
nothing to do with the future.

01:01:34.440 --> 01:01:36.107 align:middle line:84%
If it has nothing to
do with the future,

01:01:36.107 --> 01:01:38.490 align:middle line:84%
that can't-- you can't deter
entry by setting low prices

01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:41.040 align:middle line:84%
to avoid getting people to
come in and compete with you.

01:01:41.040 --> 01:01:45.270 align:middle line:84%
And so there are
several different parts

01:01:45.270 --> 01:01:48.210 align:middle line:84%
of the IO literature that
are discussed, stories

01:01:48.210 --> 01:01:50.850 align:middle line:84%
that would account
for firms wanting

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:53.760 align:middle line:84%
to price below the
static monopoly profit

01:01:53.760 --> 01:01:56.250 align:middle line:90%
in order to keep firms out.

01:01:56.250 --> 01:01:58.650 align:middle line:90%
One of them is a classic--

01:01:58.650 --> 01:02:02.370 align:middle line:84%
is Milgrom and
Roberts, 1982, noted

01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:06.630 align:middle line:84%
that you could have limit
pricing if firm two doesn't

01:02:06.630 --> 01:02:12.170 align:middle line:84%
know firm one's costs, and
prices are a signal of costs.

01:02:12.170 --> 01:02:15.650 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be a
classic signaling game.

01:02:15.650 --> 01:02:19.250 align:middle line:84%
Firm one has two possible
cost levels, C lower bar

01:02:19.250 --> 01:02:20.780 align:middle line:90%
or C upper bar.

01:02:20.780 --> 01:02:23.240 align:middle line:84%
Assume that firm
one knows its costs.

01:02:23.240 --> 01:02:26.780 align:middle line:84%
Firm two doesn't know
firm one's costs.

01:02:26.780 --> 01:02:29.720 align:middle line:84%
Firm two has some prior
that there's probability mu

01:02:29.720 --> 01:02:33.740 align:middle line:84%
that its a high-cost firm,
and 1 minus mu that firm one's

01:02:33.740 --> 01:02:35.180 align:middle line:90%
a low-cost firm.

01:02:35.180 --> 01:02:38.330 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 is going to have
some separate costs, C2,

01:02:38.330 --> 01:02:40.010 align:middle line:90%
if it comes in and competes.

01:02:40.010 --> 01:02:43.400 align:middle line:84%
It knows C2, but it doesn't know
whether firm one has a better

01:02:43.400 --> 01:02:44.910 align:middle line:90%
or worse technology than it is.

01:02:44.910 --> 01:02:48.170 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't know if
firm one is a lower cost

01:02:48.170 --> 01:02:53.050 align:middle line:84%
firm than it is, or firm one is
a higher cost firm than it is.

01:02:53.050 --> 01:02:56.020 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to think of
a two-period model, where firm

01:02:56.020 --> 01:02:59.530 align:middle line:90%
one chooses p11 at t equals 1.

01:02:59.530 --> 01:03:02.650 align:middle line:84%
Firm two observes the price
that firm one is charging,

01:03:02.650 --> 01:03:05.230 align:middle line:84%
and then it chooses
whether to enter or not

01:03:05.230 --> 01:03:08.170 align:middle line:84%
and has some fixed
entry cost, E.

01:03:08.170 --> 01:03:13.000 align:middle line:84%
And then we either
get a monopoly,

01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:17.380 align:middle line:84%
where firm one produces and
earns monopoly profits that

01:03:17.380 --> 01:03:19.870 align:middle line:90%
depend on its actual cost, C1.

01:03:19.870 --> 01:03:24.170 align:middle line:84%
Or we get a duopoly, where the
profits depend on the costs C1

01:03:24.170 --> 01:03:24.670 align:middle line:90%
and C2.

01:03:24.670 --> 01:03:28.070 align:middle line:90%


01:03:28.070 --> 01:03:30.433 align:middle line:84%
And in this model,
Milgrom and Roberts

01:03:30.433 --> 01:03:31.850 align:middle line:84%
notes there are
two different ways

01:03:31.850 --> 01:03:35.780 align:middle line:84%
we can think of where we can
get firm one in equilibrium

01:03:35.780 --> 01:03:41.270 align:middle line:84%
to set a price lower than it
would set in the blockaded entry

01:03:41.270 --> 01:03:43.300 align:middle line:90%
case.

01:03:43.300 --> 01:03:47.770 align:middle line:84%
Or in this case, it's simply
the profit-maximizing case.

01:03:47.770 --> 01:03:49.620 align:middle line:90%
And why can that be?

01:03:49.620 --> 01:03:52.360 align:middle line:90%
So there are two cases.

01:03:52.360 --> 01:04:06.040 align:middle line:84%
Case one is E, such that
firm two won't enter

01:04:06.040 --> 01:04:07.516 align:middle line:90%
if it gets no information.

01:04:07.516 --> 01:04:11.290 align:middle line:90%


01:04:11.290 --> 01:04:14.185 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that firm two--
it's unsure about where

01:04:14.185 --> 01:04:17.185 align:middle line:90%
the firm one's costs are.

01:04:17.185 --> 01:04:19.560 align:middle line:84%
We're going to typically think
of if firm one has costs C

01:04:19.560 --> 01:04:21.690 align:middle line:84%
lower bar, firm two is
going to get low profits

01:04:21.690 --> 01:04:23.320 align:middle line:90%
and wish it hadn't entered.

01:04:23.320 --> 01:04:25.350 align:middle line:84%
If C is C upper
bar, then firm two

01:04:25.350 --> 01:04:28.200 align:middle line:84%
is going to earn high profits
and be happy that it entered.

01:04:28.200 --> 01:04:30.420 align:middle line:84%
But imagine that the
balance between those two

01:04:30.420 --> 01:04:34.440 align:middle line:84%
is that if firm two doesn't
get any new information,

01:04:34.440 --> 01:04:36.690 align:middle line:84%
so it still has this
prior mu 1 minus mu,

01:04:36.690 --> 01:04:39.930 align:middle line:84%
it's going to stay
out of the market.

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:42.900 align:middle line:84%
Well, if firm two
would stay out,

01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:45.300 align:middle line:84%
if it gets no new
information, we

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:48.300 align:middle line:90%
can get a pooling equilibrium.

01:04:48.300 --> 01:04:50.160 align:middle line:84%
And the pooling
equilibrium-- firm

01:04:50.160 --> 01:04:53.160 align:middle line:84%
one is setting a low price
at t equals 1, regardless of

01:04:53.160 --> 01:04:54.760 align:middle line:90%
whether its cost is.

01:04:54.760 --> 01:04:57.300 align:middle line:84%
And then firm two stays
out if it sees this price

01:04:57.300 --> 01:05:00.050 align:middle line:90%
and enters otherwise.

01:05:00.050 --> 01:05:04.670 align:middle line:84%
So you imagine there are these
two different cost levels or two

01:05:04.670 --> 01:05:06.290 align:middle line:90%
different price levels.

01:05:06.290 --> 01:05:12.410 align:middle line:84%
P1 lower bar would be the
argmax of firm one's profits.

01:05:12.410 --> 01:05:16.340 align:middle line:84%
If it's at price P1 and
its cost to C lower bar.

01:05:16.340 --> 01:05:22.250 align:middle line:84%
And P1 upper bar would
be the argmax of pi 1--

01:05:22.250 --> 01:05:23.780 align:middle line:90%
these are monopoly profits--

01:05:23.780 --> 01:05:26.830 align:middle line:90%
at P1 C upper bar.

01:05:26.830 --> 01:05:31.570 align:middle line:84%
So these two different profit
level-- two different price

01:05:31.570 --> 01:05:36.700 align:middle line:84%
levels that you would set if
you had low cost or high cost.

01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:40.660 align:middle line:84%
But if you imagine-- if we tried
to have an equilibrium where

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:42.880 align:middle line:84%
firm one set this price
when its cost was low

01:05:42.880 --> 01:05:47.410 align:middle line:84%
and this price when its cost
was high, if you set this price,

01:05:47.410 --> 01:05:50.380 align:middle line:84%
firm two is going to realize
that you have high cost.

01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:52.360 align:middle line:84%
And then firm two
is going to enter.

01:05:52.360 --> 01:05:54.730 align:middle line:84%
So what we can get is
this pooling equilibrium,

01:05:54.730 --> 01:06:01.660 align:middle line:84%
where we have p11 equals
p1 lower bar for all C.

01:06:01.660 --> 01:06:05.650 align:middle line:84%
So if your cost is low,
you set price C lower bar.

01:06:05.650 --> 01:06:06.990 align:middle line:90%
If your cost is high--

01:06:06.990 --> 01:06:07.490 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

01:06:07.490 --> 01:06:10.460 align:middle line:84%
If you set-- price is low,
you set price P1 lower bar.

01:06:10.460 --> 01:06:14.670 align:middle line:84%
But if your cost is high, you
also set price P1 lower bar.

01:06:14.670 --> 01:06:17.130 align:middle line:84%
And so you're
pricing low because

01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:20.040 align:middle line:90%
by pricing low, firm two--

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:22.140 align:middle line:84%
firm two knows that
firm one price is low

01:06:22.140 --> 01:06:24.100 align:middle line:90%
even if it's cost is high.

01:06:24.100 --> 01:06:28.020 align:middle line:84%
But by doing that, it prevents
firm two from learning anything.

01:06:28.020 --> 01:06:30.160 align:middle line:84%
And by preventing firm to
from learning anything,

01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:31.505 align:middle line:90%
firm two isn't going to enter.

01:06:31.505 --> 01:06:38.860 align:middle line:90%


01:06:38.860 --> 01:06:42.710 align:middle line:84%
So kind of a familiar argument
how signaling models typically

01:06:42.710 --> 01:06:43.210 align:middle line:90%
work.

01:06:43.210 --> 01:06:45.085 align:middle line:84%
That is, firm two is
going to infer something

01:06:45.085 --> 01:06:46.340 align:middle line:90%
from your equilibrium play.

01:06:46.340 --> 01:06:48.160 align:middle line:84%
So we can have this
pooling equilibrium

01:06:48.160 --> 01:06:51.267 align:middle line:84%
where we all charge a low
price regardless of our costs,

01:06:51.267 --> 01:06:53.350 align:middle line:84%
and that prevents firm two
from learning anything.

01:06:53.350 --> 01:06:57.810 align:middle line:84%
That gives you limit pricing
to keep firm two out.

01:06:57.810 --> 01:07:01.175 align:middle line:84%
The other thing you can get--
and this would be the case two.

01:07:01.175 --> 01:07:05.360 align:middle line:90%


01:07:05.360 --> 01:07:14.360 align:middle line:84%
Case two is E, such
that firm two enters

01:07:14.360 --> 01:07:16.630 align:middle line:90%
if it gets no new info.

01:07:16.630 --> 01:07:19.400 align:middle line:90%


01:07:19.400 --> 01:07:22.760 align:middle line:84%
In this case, clearly, we can't
have this pooling equilibrium

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:23.300 align:middle line:90%
anymore.

01:07:23.300 --> 01:07:25.550 align:middle line:84%
Because in this
pooling equilibrium,

01:07:25.550 --> 01:07:27.590 align:middle line:84%
if this was how people
played the game,

01:07:27.590 --> 01:07:30.690 align:middle line:84%
then all the-- then firm
two wouldn't learn anything.

01:07:30.690 --> 01:07:32.700 align:middle line:84%
But then firm two
would always enter.

01:07:32.700 --> 01:07:34.310 align:middle line:84%
And so if you're
a high-cost firm,

01:07:34.310 --> 01:07:36.920 align:middle line:84%
why are you charging a low price
when it doesn't affect entry

01:07:36.920 --> 01:07:37.980 align:middle line:90%
in the future?

01:07:37.980 --> 01:07:40.350 align:middle line:84%
That's, again, this here
relevant entry case.

01:07:40.350 --> 01:07:43.910 align:middle line:84%
But so we can get this
equilibrium where we have P1--

01:07:43.910 --> 01:07:46.940 align:middle line:90%


01:07:46.940 --> 01:07:48.890 align:middle line:84%
I'll call it the limit
pricing equilibrium.

01:07:48.890 --> 01:07:58.190 align:middle line:84%
P1 limit pricing of C1 is
less than P1 lower bar.

01:07:58.190 --> 01:08:02.000 align:middle line:90%
And P1 limit pricing of C1--

01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:08.720 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- of C lower bar and of C
upper bar equals P1 upper bar.

01:08:08.720 --> 01:08:12.350 align:middle line:84%
So we can get this equilibrium
where if your cost is high,

01:08:12.350 --> 01:08:14.160 align:middle line:84%
you just admit
your cost is high.

01:08:14.160 --> 01:08:15.500 align:middle line:90%
Set a high price.

01:08:15.500 --> 01:08:19.740 align:middle line:84%
Firm two realize you have a high
price and high cost and comes in

01:08:19.740 --> 01:08:21.600 align:middle line:84%
and enters and
competes with you.

01:08:21.600 --> 01:08:27.149 align:middle line:84%
But if your cost is low, you
charge something super low,

01:08:27.149 --> 01:08:30.510 align:middle line:84%
even lower than P1
lower bar, in some sense

01:08:30.510 --> 01:08:35.399 align:middle line:90%
to prove too firm two that--

01:08:35.399 --> 01:08:38.609 align:middle line:84%
prove to firm two that
you are the low-cost firm.

01:08:38.609 --> 01:08:41.439 align:middle line:84%
And why might you need to charge
something lower than this?

01:08:41.439 --> 01:08:44.623 align:middle line:84%
Because it could be that if
you just charge P1 lower bar,

01:08:44.623 --> 01:08:46.290 align:middle line:84%
that wouldn't be
possible in equilibrium

01:08:46.290 --> 01:08:47.915 align:middle line:84%
because then the C
upper bar firm would

01:08:47.915 --> 01:08:49.740 align:middle line:90%
pretend to be a low-cost firm.

01:08:49.740 --> 01:08:54.810 align:middle line:84%
But by pricing way, way below,
even below my static best

01:08:54.810 --> 01:08:58.500 align:middle line:84%
response price, I set a price
so low that a high-cost firm

01:08:58.500 --> 01:08:59.590 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't copy me.

01:08:59.590 --> 01:09:04.060 align:middle line:84%
And then it's a credible signal
that my cost really is low.

01:09:04.060 --> 01:09:07.000 align:middle line:84%
So we can get these two
different types of equilibria--

01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:08.620 align:middle line:84%
the pooling
equilibria, where it's

01:09:08.620 --> 01:09:15.100 align:middle line:84%
the high-cost guys who price
below their static optimum.

01:09:15.100 --> 01:09:16.990 align:middle line:84%
And then the
separating equilibria,

01:09:16.990 --> 01:09:18.790 align:middle line:84%
where the low-cost
guys are pricing

01:09:18.790 --> 01:09:20.850 align:middle line:84%
below their static optimum
to signal their costs.

01:09:20.850 --> 01:09:24.430 align:middle line:90%


01:09:24.430 --> 01:09:27.130 align:middle line:84%
So that's a
cost-signaling argument

01:09:27.130 --> 01:09:31.000 align:middle line:84%
for why firms might
want-- and it's plausible

01:09:31.000 --> 01:09:33.370 align:middle line:84%
that the firm in the market
knows more about their costs

01:09:33.370 --> 01:09:35.479 align:middle line:90%
than the other firm does.

01:09:35.479 --> 01:09:37.870 align:middle line:84%
So you price low to
signal your costs.

01:09:37.870 --> 01:09:39.790 align:middle line:84%
And that is why we
see firms pricing

01:09:39.790 --> 01:09:41.630 align:middle line:84%
low to try to keep
out competition.

01:09:41.630 --> 01:09:48.790 align:middle line:90%


01:09:48.790 --> 01:09:49.300 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:09:49.300 --> 01:09:51.092 align:middle line:84%
Second kind of story
I was going to tell us

01:09:51.092 --> 01:09:56.320 align:middle line:84%
what Fudenberg and Tirole
call a signal-jamming story.

01:09:56.320 --> 01:09:59.710 align:middle line:84%
In signal-jamming
models, firms one and two

01:09:59.710 --> 01:10:02.650 align:middle line:84%
are both symmetrically
uninformed about some state

01:10:02.650 --> 01:10:04.450 align:middle line:90%
of nature.

01:10:04.450 --> 01:10:07.450 align:middle line:84%
But, again, signal jamming
can be motivated by a desire

01:10:07.450 --> 01:10:10.270 align:middle line:90%
to deter entry or induce exit.

01:10:10.270 --> 01:10:16.660 align:middle line:84%
And it, again, can look like
pricing low either to try to--

01:10:16.660 --> 01:10:19.150 align:middle line:84%
pricing low either to prevent
firm two from entering,

01:10:19.150 --> 01:10:22.097 align:middle line:84%
or to force firm two
to exit a market.

01:10:22.097 --> 01:10:23.930 align:middle line:84%
So I thought I would
do this one just like--

01:10:23.930 --> 01:10:28.190 align:middle line:84%
I've been doing everything
in terms of avoiding entry.

01:10:28.190 --> 01:10:30.730 align:middle line:84%
I figured I'd do this one
in terms of forcing exit.

01:10:30.730 --> 01:10:33.620 align:middle line:84%
But some of the same stories
can be told both kinds of games.

01:10:33.620 --> 01:10:35.080 align:middle line:90%
So here's my model.

01:10:35.080 --> 01:10:40.077 align:middle line:84%
So firms one and two choose
quantities q11 and q21

01:10:40.077 --> 01:10:40.910 align:middle line:90%
in the first period.

01:10:40.910 --> 01:10:44.730 align:middle line:84%
So I'm doing this
in a Cournot model.

01:10:44.730 --> 01:10:47.730 align:middle line:84%
There is a market demand
that determines some price.

01:10:47.730 --> 01:10:52.860 align:middle line:84%
And the market demand is theta
minus q1 plus q2, where theta is

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:54.690 align:middle line:90%
an unknown state of the world.

01:10:54.690 --> 01:10:57.840 align:middle line:84%
So neither of the firms
knows how big the market

01:10:57.840 --> 01:10:58.830 align:middle line:90%
is for their product.

01:10:58.830 --> 01:10:59.920 align:middle line:90%
It's a new product.

01:10:59.920 --> 01:11:01.420 align:middle line:90%
They've just both invented it.

01:11:01.420 --> 01:11:03.930 align:middle line:84%
They start-- they produce
some number of quantities,

01:11:03.930 --> 01:11:06.270 align:middle line:84%
dump them on a Cournot
market, and then there's

01:11:06.270 --> 01:11:09.710 align:middle line:84%
some market-clearing
price for the good.

01:11:09.710 --> 01:11:12.740 align:middle line:84%
And let's assume that
theta is unknown,

01:11:12.740 --> 01:11:14.530 align:middle line:90%
and it has some common prior.

01:11:14.530 --> 01:11:19.010 align:middle line:84%
If theta is distributed normal--
mean mu variance sigma squared.

01:11:19.010 --> 01:11:21.515 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that demand
for the products is very high.

01:11:21.515 --> 01:11:23.515 align:middle line:84%
It could be demand for
the products is very low.

01:11:23.515 --> 01:11:26.160 align:middle line:90%


01:11:26.160 --> 01:11:28.140 align:middle line:84%
And so then on
the observability,

01:11:28.140 --> 01:11:30.960 align:middle line:84%
we're going to assume that after
firms choose their first period

01:11:30.960 --> 01:11:34.540 align:middle line:84%
quantities, they know what
quantity they produced.

01:11:34.540 --> 01:11:37.590 align:middle line:84%
They don't know what quantity
their rival produced.

01:11:37.590 --> 01:11:42.310 align:middle line:84%
But they do observe what the
market-clearing price was.

01:11:42.310 --> 01:11:44.200 align:middle line:84%
And so they observe
market-clearing price,

01:11:44.200 --> 01:11:45.993 align:middle line:90%
but not the rival's quantity.

01:11:45.993 --> 01:11:48.160 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to assume
that after they observe

01:11:48.160 --> 01:11:50.530 align:middle line:84%
the market price, firm two
has this this option to exit

01:11:50.530 --> 01:11:53.170 align:middle line:84%
the market, and by
scrapping its firm

01:11:53.170 --> 01:11:56.273 align:middle line:84%
earn some value, K.
And then if firm two--

01:11:56.273 --> 01:11:57.940 align:middle line:84%
depending on what
firm two does, we then

01:11:57.940 --> 01:11:59.950 align:middle line:84%
end up with firm two
exiting in their monopoly

01:11:59.950 --> 01:12:03.222 align:middle line:84%
in the second period,
or firm two stays in,

01:12:03.222 --> 01:12:05.430 align:middle line:84%
and there's duopoly competition
in the second period.

01:12:05.430 --> 01:12:09.490 align:middle line:90%


01:12:09.490 --> 01:12:10.490 align:middle line:90%
This is a misspelling.

01:12:10.490 --> 01:12:13.030 align:middle line:84%
So the equilibrium
is going to have

01:12:13.030 --> 01:12:16.600 align:middle line:84%
q11 greater than the static
best response to q21.

01:12:16.600 --> 01:12:19.780 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is going
to be in this model,

01:12:19.780 --> 01:12:22.540 align:middle line:84%
firm one is going to
what we call signal jam

01:12:22.540 --> 01:12:26.500 align:middle line:84%
and overproduce in the first
period driving down the market

01:12:26.500 --> 01:12:28.470 align:middle line:90%
price.

01:12:28.470 --> 01:12:31.770 align:middle line:84%
And in equilibrium--
firm two always

01:12:31.770 --> 01:12:34.090 align:middle line:84%
knows what firm one is
doing in equilibrium.

01:12:34.090 --> 01:12:38.130 align:middle line:84%
So in equilibrium, firm two
correctly anticipates q11.

01:12:38.130 --> 01:12:45.180 align:middle line:84%
It can always then infer that it
knows that theta minus q11 plus

01:12:45.180 --> 01:12:48.850 align:middle line:90%
q21 equals P1.

01:12:48.850 --> 01:12:50.910 align:middle line:84%
So it can always
infer that theta

01:12:50.910 --> 01:12:57.450 align:middle line:90%
equals P1 plus q11 plus q21.

01:12:57.450 --> 01:12:59.140 align:middle line:84%
So I guess this is
the way it infers it.

01:12:59.140 --> 01:13:00.900 align:middle line:84%
It's like after
the first period,

01:13:00.900 --> 01:13:03.270 align:middle line:90%
it sees the first period price.

01:13:03.270 --> 01:13:05.670 align:middle line:84%
It knows what
quantity it produced.

01:13:05.670 --> 01:13:06.600 align:middle line:90%
It anticipates.

01:13:06.600 --> 01:13:09.250 align:middle line:84%
It knows the
equilibrium, q11 star.

01:13:09.250 --> 01:13:10.800 align:middle line:84%
And so it now
believes that theta

01:13:10.800 --> 01:13:13.770 align:middle line:90%
equals this with probability 1.

01:13:13.770 --> 01:13:19.900 align:middle line:84%
And so result is that
this equilibrium has

01:13:19.900 --> 01:13:24.060 align:middle line:84%
to have overproduction at
t equals 1 by firm one.

01:13:24.060 --> 01:13:24.970 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

01:13:24.970 --> 01:13:27.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, because
suppose that firm one

01:13:27.330 --> 01:13:31.760 align:middle line:84%
was just playing the static
best response to q21.

01:13:31.760 --> 01:13:34.970 align:middle line:84%
Then firm one could increase
its output with no first order

01:13:34.970 --> 01:13:36.950 align:middle line:90%
loss and profits.

01:13:36.950 --> 01:13:39.470 align:middle line:84%
And there's a one
for one relationship

01:13:39.470 --> 01:13:42.530 align:middle line:84%
between the more I produce
in the first period

01:13:42.530 --> 01:13:48.055 align:middle line:84%
and what firm two thinks
theta is going to be.

01:13:48.055 --> 01:13:58.160 align:middle line:90%


01:13:58.160 --> 01:13:59.510 align:middle line:90%
Because if I produce--

01:13:59.510 --> 01:14:05.300 align:middle line:84%
imagine that I produce
q11 greater than q1 star.

01:14:05.300 --> 01:14:08.030 align:middle line:84%
Firm two is thinking
I produce q1 star.

01:14:08.030 --> 01:14:13.100 align:middle line:84%
So when I produce q11,
P1 gets driven down.

01:14:13.100 --> 01:14:15.920 align:middle line:84%
And so when P1 gets
driven down, P1

01:14:15.920 --> 01:14:18.740 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
actually the true P1.

01:14:18.740 --> 01:14:20.675 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's going
to be the-- sorry.

01:14:20.675 --> 01:14:24.890 align:middle line:84%
What I'm going to get is their
estimated theta, theta hat,

01:14:24.890 --> 01:14:31.400 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the true theta
minus q11 minus q1 star.

01:14:31.400 --> 01:14:39.390 align:middle line:84%
So if I overproduce, firm two is
going to miss estimate theta 2

01:14:39.390 --> 01:14:41.220 align:middle line:84%
and think theta 2 is
lower than it really

01:14:41.220 --> 01:14:43.980 align:middle line:84%
is because the price
was lower than it should

01:14:43.980 --> 01:14:45.850 align:middle line:90%
have been given this q11 star.

01:14:45.850 --> 01:14:49.210 align:middle line:84%
So firm two is going to
think theta is lower.

01:14:49.210 --> 01:14:53.020 align:middle line:84%
And so because firm two thinks
theta is lower than it really

01:14:53.020 --> 01:14:55.210 align:middle line:84%
is, firm two is going to
be more likely to exit

01:14:55.210 --> 01:14:57.380 align:middle line:90%
after the first period.

01:14:57.380 --> 01:14:59.540 align:middle line:84%
But then if firm two
is more likely to exit

01:14:59.540 --> 01:15:04.320 align:middle line:84%
in the first period, that's good
for me, and so I want to do it.

01:15:04.320 --> 01:15:06.670 align:middle line:84%
So why would I not do
this in equilibrium?

01:15:06.670 --> 01:15:08.670 align:middle line:84%
The only reason I would
not do it in equilibrium

01:15:08.670 --> 01:15:14.400 align:middle line:84%
is choosing q11 greater than
q1 star has a first order

01:15:14.400 --> 01:15:15.870 align:middle line:90%
cost to me.

01:15:15.870 --> 01:15:18.370 align:middle line:84%
And the only way it's going to
have a first order cost to me

01:15:18.370 --> 01:15:22.493 align:middle line:84%
is if q11 is greater than the
static best response to q21.

01:15:22.493 --> 01:15:25.110 align:middle line:90%


01:15:25.110 --> 01:15:29.020 align:middle line:84%
So what happened is we have this
equilibrium where I overproduce.

01:15:29.020 --> 01:15:30.398 align:middle line:90%
He knows I'm overproducing.

01:15:30.398 --> 01:15:32.190 align:middle line:84%
He correctly infers
what's going to happen,

01:15:32.190 --> 01:15:33.670 align:middle line:90%
given the overproduction.

01:15:33.670 --> 01:15:36.660 align:middle line:84%
But the overproduction by me
is necessary to have me not

01:15:36.660 --> 01:15:39.600 align:middle line:84%
have an incentive to
overproduce by even more.

01:15:39.600 --> 01:15:41.970 align:middle line:84%
And so we get this
signal-jamming equilibrium

01:15:41.970 --> 01:15:45.767 align:middle line:84%
where firm one produces
more than it looks

01:15:45.767 --> 01:15:47.850 align:middle line:84%
like the static best
response in the first period,

01:15:47.850 --> 01:15:49.380 align:middle line:90%
drives prices down.

01:15:49.380 --> 01:15:53.070 align:middle line:84%
And it drives prices down in an
attempt to get firm two to exit.

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:55.230 align:middle line:84%
And notice, there is
a funny efficiency

01:15:55.230 --> 01:15:57.660 align:middle line:84%
thing behind that story
is in equilibrium,

01:15:57.660 --> 01:16:01.050 align:middle line:84%
firm two anticipates
theta correctly,

01:16:01.050 --> 01:16:03.270 align:middle line:84%
undoes my signal
jamming, and only

01:16:03.270 --> 01:16:05.990 align:middle line:84%
exits when it would
exit efficiently anyway.

01:16:05.990 --> 01:16:07.800 align:middle line:84%
So this signal-jamming
is, in some sense,

01:16:07.800 --> 01:16:09.870 align:middle line:84%
good for social welfare
relative to the world

01:16:09.870 --> 01:16:13.300 align:middle line:84%
where all the cues were
available-- were observable.

01:16:13.300 --> 01:16:15.330 align:middle line:84%
Because if the cues
were observable,

01:16:15.330 --> 01:16:17.230 align:middle line:84%
you'd get exactly
the same entry.

01:16:17.230 --> 01:16:18.690 align:middle line:84%
Except here, we
get this low price,

01:16:18.690 --> 01:16:20.190 align:middle line:84%
and then we still
get the same entry

01:16:20.190 --> 01:16:22.150 align:middle line:90%
you'd get with observable cues.

01:16:22.150 --> 01:16:27.800 align:middle line:84%
So this unobservability is
just socially a good thing.

01:16:27.800 --> 01:16:31.010 align:middle line:84%
I guess, one more
classic paper--

01:16:31.010 --> 01:16:33.410 align:middle line:90%
Aghion and Bolton, 1987--

01:16:33.410 --> 01:16:36.770 align:middle line:84%
discusses the
question of, can firms

01:16:36.770 --> 01:16:40.670 align:middle line:84%
deter entry by getting
their customers

01:16:40.670 --> 01:16:42.720 align:middle line:90%
to sign long-term contracts?

01:16:42.720 --> 01:16:44.090 align:middle line:90%
Like, if I could get--

01:16:44.090 --> 01:16:46.730 align:middle line:84%
if I'm trying to deter
entry by rival cell phone

01:16:46.730 --> 01:16:49.453 align:middle line:84%
companies in Boston,
could I just get

01:16:49.453 --> 01:16:50.870 align:middle line:84%
everyone who's
currently in Boston

01:16:50.870 --> 01:16:53.780 align:middle line:84%
to sign like, say, my
cell phone contracts

01:16:53.780 --> 01:16:55.010 align:middle line:90%
have a five-year commitment.

01:16:55.010 --> 01:16:56.130 align:middle line:90%
You sign up with me.

01:16:56.130 --> 01:16:57.360 align:middle line:90%
It's a five-year commitment.

01:16:57.360 --> 01:17:00.920 align:middle line:84%
You have to pay me an early
termination fee of $2,000 if you

01:17:00.920 --> 01:17:02.610 align:middle line:90%
leave me before the five years.

01:17:02.610 --> 01:17:04.670 align:middle line:84%
Can I use lockup
long-term contracts

01:17:04.670 --> 01:17:08.270 align:middle line:84%
like that to deter
entry by rivals?

01:17:08.270 --> 01:17:12.320 align:middle line:84%
And, again, the Chicago school
argument was always, well, no.

01:17:12.320 --> 01:17:14.210 align:middle line:84%
You can't use
long-term contracts

01:17:14.210 --> 01:17:17.000 align:middle line:84%
to deter entry because
the customer wouldn't

01:17:17.000 --> 01:17:18.560 align:middle line:84%
sign the long-term
contract unless it

01:17:18.560 --> 01:17:20.190 align:middle line:90%
was in their best interest.

01:17:20.190 --> 01:17:24.110 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, we
shouldn't think

01:17:24.110 --> 01:17:27.900 align:middle line:84%
that firms can use long-term
contracts to deter entry

01:17:27.900 --> 01:17:30.570 align:middle line:84%
because the customers wouldn't
sign the contracts unless it

01:17:30.570 --> 01:17:33.750 align:middle line:84%
was in their interest
to sign the contracts.

01:17:33.750 --> 01:17:36.450 align:middle line:90%
Two problems with that story.

01:17:36.450 --> 01:17:42.930 align:middle line:84%
One is the Chicago story may
make sense for large buyers

01:17:42.930 --> 01:17:44.910 align:middle line:90%
or monopoly buyers.

01:17:44.910 --> 01:17:46.650 align:middle line:84%
But it certainly
doesn't make sense

01:17:46.650 --> 01:17:48.510 align:middle line:84%
in markets like the
cell phone market,

01:17:48.510 --> 01:17:52.800 align:middle line:84%
where you have a large number
of infinitesimal consumers.

01:17:52.800 --> 01:17:59.040 align:middle line:84%
Because you-- you
graduate student at MIT,

01:17:59.040 --> 01:18:00.160 align:middle line:90%
you walk into Verizon.

01:18:00.160 --> 01:18:02.130 align:middle line:84%
They say, we only offer
five-year contracts.

01:18:02.130 --> 01:18:02.850 align:middle line:84%
You walk to T-Mobile,
and they say,

01:18:02.850 --> 01:18:04.080 align:middle line:84%
we only offer
five-year contracts.

01:18:04.080 --> 01:18:05.190 align:middle line:90%
So you're like, I'm not--

01:18:05.190 --> 01:18:07.440 align:middle line:84%
I don't even know I'm going
to be here for five years.

01:18:07.440 --> 01:18:09.540 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to sign
a five-year contract.

01:18:09.540 --> 01:18:11.040 align:middle line:84%
Give me a one-year
contract instead.

01:18:11.040 --> 01:18:12.720 align:middle line:90%
They're going to say, fine.

01:18:12.720 --> 01:18:13.570 align:middle line:90%
Don't buy a phone.

01:18:13.570 --> 01:18:14.280 align:middle line:90%
And you're going to say.

01:18:14.280 --> 01:18:15.738 align:middle line:84%
I want to buy the
one-year contract

01:18:15.738 --> 01:18:19.050 align:middle line:84%
because I know that if all
of us buy one-year contracts,

01:18:19.050 --> 01:18:21.520 align:middle line:84%
some third firm is going
to enter the Boston market.

01:18:21.520 --> 01:18:23.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, the answer is you,
as a graduate student,

01:18:23.610 --> 01:18:24.880 align:middle line:90%
that's not happening.

01:18:24.880 --> 01:18:26.610 align:middle line:84%
Like, if there was
a monopoly buyer

01:18:26.610 --> 01:18:29.460 align:middle line:84%
who could commit to I'm only
signing a one-year contract,

01:18:29.460 --> 01:18:32.080 align:middle line:84%
then someone's going to
enter, that would be better.

01:18:32.080 --> 01:18:36.720 align:middle line:84%
But you-- if you know that
everybody else is signing

01:18:36.720 --> 01:18:38.982 align:middle line:84%
the five-year contract,
you holding out and not

01:18:38.982 --> 01:18:41.440 align:middle line:84%
signing the five-year contract
is not going to do anything.

01:18:41.440 --> 01:18:42.780 align:middle line:90%
It's not going to affect entry.

01:18:42.780 --> 01:18:44.790 align:middle line:90%
So there's a free rider problem.

01:18:44.790 --> 01:18:47.640 align:middle line:84%
Even if it's bad for all
the consumers in aggregate,

01:18:47.640 --> 01:18:49.830 align:middle line:84%
you could sign it because
of the free rider problem.

01:18:49.830 --> 01:18:52.980 align:middle line:90%


01:18:52.980 --> 01:18:59.310 align:middle line:84%
Aghion and Bolton note that
even with a monopoly buyer,

01:18:59.310 --> 01:19:02.910 align:middle line:84%
we can still tell stories where
the long-term contracts get

01:19:02.910 --> 01:19:06.970 align:middle line:84%
signed even though they're not
optimal or not socially optimal.

01:19:06.970 --> 01:19:10.110 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that
the long-term contracts

01:19:10.110 --> 01:19:13.080 align:middle line:84%
are in some sense
stealing money from firm

01:19:13.080 --> 01:19:14.710 align:middle line:90%
two, the potential entrant.

01:19:14.710 --> 01:19:17.790 align:middle line:84%
And you, in some sense, take
that business stealing profit

01:19:17.790 --> 01:19:20.850 align:middle line:84%
and divide it among the
customer signing the contract

01:19:20.850 --> 01:19:22.350 align:middle line:90%
and the initial monopolist.

01:19:22.350 --> 01:19:27.490 align:middle line:84%
And by stealing the profits
away from the future firm two,

01:19:27.490 --> 01:19:29.970 align:middle line:84%
you can divide those up
among and get a situation

01:19:29.970 --> 01:19:33.400 align:middle line:84%
where it's bad socially to
have these long-term tie-ups.

01:19:33.400 --> 01:19:36.300 align:middle line:84%
But the monopolist
wants to offer them,

01:19:36.300 --> 01:19:38.160 align:middle line:84%
and the customers
want to sign them.

01:19:38.160 --> 01:19:41.740 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I will not go
through that argument.

01:19:41.740 --> 01:19:43.340 align:middle line:90%
It's written there.

01:19:43.340 --> 01:19:44.170 align:middle line:90%
I had one more.

01:19:44.170 --> 01:19:44.670 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

01:19:44.670 --> 01:19:46.410 align:middle line:84%
So Wednesday, I'm
going to go back

01:19:46.410 --> 01:19:49.230 align:middle line:84%
to empirical papers on
strategic investment.

01:19:49.230 --> 01:19:52.890 align:middle line:84%
I think these three are
the ones that I'm mostly

01:19:52.890 --> 01:19:55.240 align:middle line:90%
going to talk about.

01:19:55.240 --> 01:19:55.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:19:55.930 --> 01:19:57.780 align:middle line:90%
See you then.

01:19:57.780 --> 01:20:04.000 align:middle line:90%